{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"# Call-site provenance: occurrence contract and durable correspondence\n\nStatus: architect design; no implementation or adoption acceptance claimed.\nAuthority: signed Addendum A v3 and signed attribution ruling v4.\nScope: read-only source investigation; this findings document is the sole output.\nDesign revision: 1 (2026-11-06).\n\nCitation convention: paths beginning `yang/` or `yin/` are under `src/cljc/`; a shortened filename inherits the nearest explicit namespace/path. `ruling-of-record.md` abbreviates the signed 1794600000000 ruling named in §1; `code-as-tuples.md` abbreviates `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`. Line ranges identify inspected source, while normative decisions in C1/D1 and the acceptance gates are new design requirements.\n\n## 1. Decision and evidence limits\n\nAdopt one language-neutral application-occurrence contract over the existing occurrence relation, one reserved frontend-metadata key, and the already-ruled third trace field. Keep code content, provenance, attribution evidence, and verification results separate. Publish the durable correspondence as an additional addressable record referring to the oracle's evidence **without changing that evidence's format**. Production adoption includes Clojure, TypeScript, PHP, Python, their preludes, and the additional ingress paths inventoried below.\n\nThe authority is `collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-RECORD.md:13-29` (A1–A4 and measurements), and `collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md:11-73` (R1–R7). In particular, signed A4 at line 25 explicitly strikes “or atomically with”: **implementation AND independent reconstruction must pass BEFORE acceptance of the first additional relying consumer**. Simultaneous code landing is possible; simultaneous acceptance before reconstruction is not. The oracle remains unblocked.\n\nRead order was addendum, architectural foundations, signed base ruling, then the recorded Astra addendum review. Astra's final review is the JSON agent-message at `1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log:48`; signed v3 supersedes its earlier “before, or atomically with” wording. No separate qwen addendum review was present in `collab/yang-clojure/` during this inspection. The available `1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md` is a base-ruling review, not the missing addendum review. Signed v3 records incorporation of qwen's amendments at lines 3–4.\n\nSource inspection occurred in a live workspace: master initially reported `e158afca`, later `5854869c5117e3659d8ce3bc3f140d017d7b8687`. Citations below identify inspected file locations, not a claim that all work was read from an immutable commit. Rebase/inventory refresh after the cell-ref sweeps and J2 is an implementation prerequisite. No tests, compilers, generators, or publication commands were run for this design.\n\n### 1.1 The oracle format is not yet available to freeze\n\nThe inspected worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-aaq`, branch `attribution-as-query`, reports HEAD `3eebe844e406ba56bc38a5f4b4a0098d811b5efd`. Its tracked `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1794-1839` still generates two-field body-entry traces and uses the offset accounting at lines 1786–1819. Its `BRIEF-aaq.md:15-21` requires the future inventory, three-field instrumentation, and versioned mechanically derived site tables; it does not define their schema. Untracked `probe/aaq_probe.clj:17-29` uses exploratory map-key paths, not the canonical row-position paths; it is not a published contract. The local dispatch log `collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log:1` reports an unrecognized-model error. This does not establish the status of work elsewhere.\n\nConsequently **the oracle evidence schema's exact identifier, field names, and canonical-source-hash algorithm/profile are unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides from the oracle's accepted record**. This document specifies the envelope around that record and its verification obligations, not an independently invented replacement site-table schema. “OracleEvidence” below means the entire accepted versioned oracle value, verbatim, including its site ids, argument-shape declarations, fixture linkage, trace schema, and version. Before durable implementation is accepted, the inventory must pin its concrete file, commit, schema identifier/version, validator, and derivation entry point. Absence of that pin blocks durable acceptance; it does not block the oracle round.\n\nThis is the format-race control demanded by A4. An implementation must not adopt a hypothetical table in this document or mistake `throw_sites.edn` for the new caller-site table.\n\n## 2. Occurrence contract C1\n\nThe following is the proposed normative contract, grounded in `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:393-455,658-665,918-931`.\n\n### 2.1 Identity and scope\n\nAn application site is `K = [origin root-address path]`.\n\n- `root-address` is the canonical root of the particular final UAST tree being named, not the application's subtree address or an instrumented runtime-map hash.\n- `path` is the grammar's structural path: scalar child slot steps are row positions; a child in a `nodes` slot is `[slot-position member-index]`. The root is `[]`. Never substitute map keys, host iteration order, allocation ids, source line numbers, PCs, callee names, or arities.\n- `origin` identifies the actual admission or generation event. Admitted syntax uses `[:source medium batch-token j]`; generated syntax uses `[:expansion expansion-record-address]`. Generated post-expansion trees must not be mislabeled as original source or given a fabricated expansion address. If a no-op expansion retains an admitted tree, the original source origin remains valid; “post-expansion” names the pipeline boundary, not proof that a generating event occurred.\n- An origin is supplied by the admitting/generating composition. Two separately admitted identical sources have different origins; two identical applications in one tree have different paths; two expansion attempts with identical output have different producing records. Replaying a single recorded admission is different from admitting the same text again.\n\nThe implemented origin validator accepts exactly the source and expansion shapes (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:938-958`). `vm/occurrences` returns `[root path node-id]`, one per place, with origin fixed by the composition; it does not return full keys or count only distinct row ids (`vm.cljc:1589-1609`).\n\nCoverage is **every final `:application` occurrence**, including generated wrappers, adapters, dispatcher/helper calls, bundled preludes, imported preludes, recursion, and statically indirect applications. “Source call” and “attributable upstream call” are projections over this coverage, not filters that delete other occurrences. Rule R's `yin/def` is structurally an application but lowers to `:define`; it gets an occurrence annotation and never a fictitious callee-entry event (`semantic/linearize.cljc:106-116`).\n\nDedicated operations such as `:dao.stream.apply/call`, stream nodes, and continuation nodes retain existing instruction provenance. They are not silently counted as `:application` sites. Their adapters' application occurrences are covered; an extension to count other node kinds must explicitly version the coverage policy (`vm.cljc:1015-1028`; `semantic/linearize.cljc:126-139`).\n\n### 2.2 Minimal persisted annotation\n\nReserve `:yang/call-site` in the existing frontend-metadata relation:\n\n```clojure\n[origin root-address path :yang/call-site 1]\n```\n\nThe integer `1` versions this key's coverage/interpretation contract. The first three coordinates ARE the site id; the value does not repeat it. This key is distinct from existing `:yang/site`, whose meaning is safepoint marks (`src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:55-75`). It carries no language name, source arity, ambient-context classification, or callee identity. Those are consumer evidence derived by the oracle or another explicitly versioned consumer.\n\nThe marker certifies that a producer preserved this occurrence under C1; the application predicate itself remains derived from canonical rows. There is no new canonical fact asserting what the grammar already says. A consumer can derive the eligible application set and compare it with the complete marker set. Under a C1 publication claim the sets must be equal; mere absence of annotations in an older publication is not false evidence of zero applications.\n\nEmit precisely one logical marker per eligible occurrence. Duplicate identical transport deliveries may be deduplicated as the same immutable fact; duplicate entries in a declared canonical table, conflicting values, unsupported versions, nonexistent paths, non-application targets, and missing/extra rows under a complete-coverage claim are validation failures. Other frontend metadata is preserved independently.\n\nImplementation may add this marker to temporary frontend map nodes before side-table projection, since the current extractor recognizes namespace `yang` (`vm.cljc:1183-1214`). Prefer deriving the relation directly from validated rows where map attachment would be redundant. `ast->semantic-bytecode` hashes only grammar slots and drops other node keys (`vm.cljc:1113-1123`); no grammar slot, instruction operand, canonical datom slot, or AST contract revision is authorized by C1.\n\n### 2.3 Finalization and transformations\n\nFinalize paths only after macro expansion, generated-name canonicalization, wrappers, module/SCC assembly, and tail marking that determine the named tree. Source-level annotations alone cannot cover generated calls (base ruling R2 at line 17). Low-level functions returning fragments may defer finalization until an owning root and origin exist; each executable/publication ingress must invoke the common finalizer. A frontend cannot claim adoption by marking just its parser or user-call lowering arm.\n\nA subsequent tree-changing transform creates another root and another occurrence relation. It must preserve an explicit old/new occurrence correspondence where it makes an attribution claim, recompute C1 for new applications, and identify generated helper calls separately. It must not retain stale paths or equate every new helper with the nearest source call. If a transform deletes, duplicates, or merges occurrences, its correspondence must record the actual multiplicity; ambiguity refuses attribution.\n\nMacro expansion already records input root, call path, macro root, parent, output and attempt (`src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:997-1000,1062-1119`). However, final tail marking and stand-in lowering happen later (`:1255-1260`). The implementation inventory must resolve how final published roots are related to these expansion outputs. Whether the existing event/record adapters already provide the complete final-root correspondence is **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides**. Do not assume an intermediate output record identifies every occurrence in the final assembled tree, and do not mint a fake `:expand` merely to satisfy the shape.\n\n### 2.4 Lowering, reconstruction, and runtime lookup\n\nPersist the existing instruction relation `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`. The row linearizer already returns `{:address ... :provenance [[pc origin root path] ...]}`; its caller supplies the segment coordinate (`semantic/linearize.cljc:188-214`; code-as-tuples §5.3 at lines 918–931). A call dispatch looks up its **own call instruction's** emitting occurrence, not the preceding operand's instruction or the callee's body. Multiple instructions may belong to one occurrence.\n\nSegment+PC alone is insufficient when identical code is loaded under two origins. The runtime must retain its load/admission origin alongside its image reference when selecting provenance. This is an occurrence-scoped runtime context, not a new code hash. The native image derivations must supply or independently reconstruct a mapping from their call PCs back through semantic PCs to K; PC equality across formats must not be assumed. Current semantic `resolve` preserves PCs, register derivation preserves layout, and stack derivation emits a new sequence (`semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-93,154-172`).\n\nCanonical reconstruction deliberately shares nodes by content (`vm.cljc:1477-1486,1522-1525`). Never cache an occurrence annotation by node-id or mutate a shared canonical reconstruction and expect distinct sites to survive. Keep an occurrence cursor/side lookup, or expand occurrence-specific ephemeral images. The datom linearizer currently returns `[pc entity]`, not K (`semantic/linearize.cljc:283-302`); use an explicit entity-to-occurrence bridge with the owning batch, root and path, refusing ambiguity. `encoder/datom-entity-occurrences` and the envelope's `:yin/entity-occurrences` are the starting machinery (`encoder.cljc:67,115-148`).\n\n### 2.5 Ephemeral origin and trace field\n\nName the nonpersistent domain **ephemeral instrumentation origin**. Its C1 spelling is:\n\n```clojure\n[:ephemeral evidence-scope-token compilation-token member-index]\n;; ephemeral site:\n[ephemeral-origin canonical-compiled-root structural-path]\n;; already signed trace shape:\n[fname arity caller-site-id]\n```\n\nThe composition supplies two non-nil plain-data tokens: `evidence-scope-token` names the recording/fixture scope; `compilation-token` names this particular instrumented compilation in that scope. `member-index` is a nonnegative integer. They are explicitly recorded inputs, not inferred from source hash, host object identity, time, lane name, or callee. Both oracle C lanes execute the SAME compiled artifact with the same tokens; a separate compilation gets another token. Replay uses the recorded tokens. A source hash alone would collapse distinct attempts.\n\nThis is a precise proposed spelling for the still-unfrozen oracle implementation. It is **not** a third legal persisted occurrence origin: `vm/occurrence-origin?` must continue rejecting it. An EDN evidence file may preserve an ephemeral id as evidence of that run; that does not convert it into a portable code occurrence. Persisted C1 side tables and expansion links require real ruled origins.\n\nThe canonical compiled root is computed after code-producing instrumentation that changes the AST, before occurrence stamps are associated into runtime maps. Runtime stamping must not introduce a root-hash self-reference. If instrumentation subsequently changes structure, it must carry an explicit transform mapping or recompute the frozen site table; no path can silently retain its old meaning.\n\nThe oracle must finalize the spelling in its own versioned evidence contract and use that format once. If its accepted schema differs by the time implementation starts, preserve its evidence verbatim and publish a reviewed binding of its origin fields to this semantic domain; do not rewrite its tables behind an unchanged version. A semantic conflict (no compilation scope, map-key paths, callee ids) requires correction on the oracle's record, not a silent normalization in durable verification.\n\nRuntime `assoc` stamping is explicitly licensed by §2.6, owner ruling 2026-09-15: the walker holds an ephemeral image and nothing runtime-associated is projected back to canonical rows; runtime keys must be stripped at serialization/projection of continuation frames (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:459-474`). Static frontend marker extraction is a separate, pre-execution side-table operation. This clause licenses oracle-specific walker instrumentation; it does not establish ordinary production coverage.\n\nCapture the caller K at actual application dispatch, after operator/operand evaluation, and preserve it until that callee's body-entry record. Nested argument calls must not overwrite it. Tail dispatch carries the new caller K even if it reuses a frame. A helper's call carries the helper's own K; no automatic inheritance of upstream credit. Unknown caller linkage yields no credit. Repeated recursion can record the same site many times; R3's consumer is existence-based, not a proof of invocation-by-invocation causality (`ruling-of-record.md:17-34`).\n\n## 3. A2 inventory: revisions, preservation, and refusal\n\nThis is a design inventory of the reachable surfaces found in the inspected sources. The implementation's first deliverable refreshes file/symbol locations after the prerequisite merges, expands each wildcard artifact family into an exact list, and assigns every item “changed”, “read/verified”, or “not reached with proof”. Newly discovered producers cannot be excluded merely because this table predates their merge.\n\n### 3.1 Contract revision decisions\n\n| Contract | Decision for this design | Revision trigger and mismatch behavior |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 frontend metadata | New reserved key/value version 1 and coverage validator. Existing five-coordinate relation is unchanged. | Unknown marker version or malformed/incomplete claimed annotation refuses C1-dependent attribution. Ordinary legacy code loading remains available. |\n| Oracle evidence | The oracle owns the new versioned site-table and three-field trace contract. Durable work imports it verbatim. | Two-field evidence is not silently upgraded to three fields. Wrong schema/hash/fixture/lane/site-shape refuses attribution. Preserve old evidence as historical data. |\n| Durable correspondence | New separate schema 1 below. | Unknown schema, missing evidence, failed reconstruction or ambiguous linkage refuses reliance, independently of code loadability. |\n| UAST/row/datom grammar | Read and validate; no C1 change. Current AST stamp is `v3`. | Existing `check-contract!` and structural/hash checks remain. Do not add a canonical slot or broaden origins to accept ephemeral ids. |\n| Semantic/stack/register execution contracts | Read and validate; no instruction-grammar change specified. Current stamps are `v4`, `b2`, `r2`. | Revise only if implementation changes instruction grammar or execution semantics. A new trace field in a harness stream alone does not revise these. |\n| Code lowering profiles | Read, and verify unchanged code output. A separately versioned provenance recipe names any new side mapping. | Revise the relevant code profile if a pinned lowering rule or emitted code changes, even if execution grammar does not. A side-only addition must not masquerade as validation by the old derivation verifier. |\n| Linker manifest | Keep schema 1 by publishing correspondence beside it. | Adding a manifest field requires a separately versioned manifest contract and validator; current closed keys reject it. No automatic schema-2 just because R1 is now active. |\n| Existing `:derive` record | Read/reference; do not add correspondence keys to it. | Current closed key set rejects extra fields and non-`:derive` ops. Reusing the record-format container for a richer correspondence would require a new/extended format, not an incidental extra field. |\n| Frontend catalog revision/profile | Change the frontend revision and relevant annotation/support profile when its publication/coverage promise changes. | Existing revision pinning and conflict/incompatible refusal remain; keep SPI 1 unless its actual request/response protocol changes. |\n| Safepoint, site-set and binder contracts | Read; preserve distinct purpose and identities. | Revise only if their serialized content/selection/transform rules change. Do not alias `:yang/call-site` to `:yang/site`. |\n| Continuation/UCF/checkpoint contracts | Read/inventory initially. | If a provenance-dependent consumer requires new serialized runtime state, inventory and version that actual state contract before shipping. A transient field cannot be silently serialized or relied on after migration. |\n| Parser/CST and `yin.cic.spec` | Read only for C1. | No parser grammar/CST revision or CIC v1 change follows merely from side metadata; R4 explicitly leaves CIC v1 untouched. |\n\nCurrent constants: `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:262-285`. Some design prose still says semantic `v3` (`docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1879-1884`); implementation inventory must use the current source stamp, not copy stale prose. Revision principles: `code-as-tuples.md:859-913`, `yin.vm.linker.md:1871-1921`. Concrete closed validators: `linker.cljc:731-759,841-871`. Frontend revision semantics: `yang/frontend.cljc:15-16,87-98,166-200`.\n\nWhether some existing higher-level ledger/publication mechanism can carry this new correspondence without **its own** schema change is **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides**. The narrower question is answered: the inspected linker's `record-defect` cannot accept extra keys under today's `:yin.ledger/record`. This design avoids making that compatibility claim.\n\n### 3.2 Producer-to-consumer preservation inventory\n\n| Surface and inspected locations | Required implementation work / read-only dependency |\n|---|---|\n| Clojure `compile-form`, `compile`, `compile-program`, `lower-unit`: `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2021,2164,2839,2993` | Cover native and argument-vector/outcome compilation, library/adapters, linked unit wrapping and global publication. Finalize at executable roots, after expansion. Existing fragment APIs need not invent origins. |\n| TypeScript packet/body/unit/multi-unit/stage: `yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:2262-2363` | Cover bundled and linked runtime paths, canonical names and tail marks, all units and generated runtime calls. Preserve packet/source association separately. |\n| PHP ANTLR body/program/packet/module/SCC/stage: `yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3737,3759,3800,3840,3855,3929,3998-4041` | Cover includes, class-map units, strict-types variants, SCC bundles/aliases, helpers and linked/bundled paths. |\n| Legacy PHP `compile-program/compile`: `yang/php.cljc:640-685` | Additional live frontend, not retired by the presence of ANTLR. Annotate all generated combinator/wrapper applications too. |\n| Python module/body/packet/SCC/stage: `yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:2018,2030,2097,2139,2198,2298,2334-2373`; session `repl.cljc:86-108` | Cover stateless and already-primed sessions, imports, bundles/aliases and generated body wrappers; first-session prelude coverage cannot stand in for subsequent submissions. |\n| REPL frontend catalog: `yin/repl/frontends.cljc:24-110` | Covers single-form vs multi-form Clojure, legacy PHP, Python session lowering and Jolt native parser selection. Standard catalog does not currently register TypeScript; direct TS paths still require adoption. |\n| Common UAST/prelude/call/module emitters: `yang/core/prelude.cljc:44-69,138-159`, `yang/core/call.cljc:184-191`, `yang/core/module.cljc:31-99` | These produce wrapper/helper/prelude code outside source-call arms. Apply common finalization at the owning publication, including direct hand-built UAST accepted as frontend output. |\n| Common streaming stage: `yang/core/stage.cljc:25-59` | It currently supplies medium plus packet unit as batch token. Audit distinct re-admissions of the same unit: C1 requires fresh admission identity while retry keeps the token; packet identity alone is insufficient if reused for distinct admissions. |\n| Encoder map/rows/datoms: `yin/vm/encoder.cljc:25-43,67-148,230-258` | Map projection emits side tables; already-projected rows currently return empty tables; envelope projection replaces table fields. Preserve/validate supplied metadata and finalize generated output instead of silently dropping it. Maintain batch member j. |\n| Macro pipeline: `yin/vm/macro.cljc:997-1005,1062-1119,1196-1261` | Keep expansion attempt provenance, resolve final-root ownership, annotate generated occurrences and retain retrievable expansion records. Post-expansion is a required integration point. |\n| Safepoint insertion: `yang/safepoint.cljc:234-256` | Current derived envelope explicitly clears frontend metadata, source positions and entity occurrences. New root needs fresh C1 plus verified input/output mapping; preserve the safepoint record's separate semantics. |\n| Projection and row validation: `yin/vm.cljc:1113-1159,1217-1259,1262-1456,1589-1609` | Reuse grammar traversal, plain-data checks and occurrence enumeration. Add a side-table coverage validator, not C1 requirements to canonical row validation. Unannotated legacy rows remain valid. |\n| Canonical and datom reconstruction: `yin/vm.cljc:705,818-851,1477-1555` | Side metadata does not reappear from content alone. Attach provenance context out of band; do not collapse occurrence ids via shared reconstruction/entity ids. Round-trip canonical hashes must remain equal. |\n| Semantic row and datom lowering: `yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc:106-128,188-221,283-302` | Persist returned provenance, supply origins explicitly, bridge datom entities with occurrences; nil-origin legacy calls remain loadable but cannot satisfy C1 attribution. |\n| Native lowering: `yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-120,154-184` | Produce/reconstruct complete native-call-PC provenance; compose with semantic mapping. Sparse variable/closure diagnostics do not establish call coverage. Inventory any legacy direct de Bruijn lowering still reached after merges. |\n| Runtime dispatch and resume: `yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:273-332,493,625-711,776`; `yin/vm/semantic.cljc:102,142,333-380` | Cold/hot application routes, operator/operand frames, linked image attachment and resumption all need occurrence context for traced execution. Native engines and UCF lifting must be audited too; no claim they already preserve caller K. |\n| Existing derivation verification: `yin/vm/ledger.cljc:156-215`, `yin/vm/linker.cljc:2164-2255` | Existing verification re-lowers code and compares identities, not side metadata. Keep code verification and add independent provenance/correspondence verification. `:trusted` does not satisfy this new acceptance gate. |\n| Generic publication: `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:115-218` | Currently takes only `:vector` from `project-rows` at line 157. Publish complete side artifacts and origin/record closure beside code; do not discard provenance or change manifest keys casually. Validate before making a C1 availability claim. |\n| Unit publication: `yang/core/publication.cljc:92-170`, PHP `publication.cljc:27-70`, Python `publication.cljc:27-80` | Publish records for actual bundle roots; aliases cannot borrow unrelated paths. Coordinate side artifacts with index/discovery publication. PHP currently has nil site-set/binder hooks; Python's site-set hook is safepoint-specific. |\n| Site-set discovery/signing precedent: `yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:5-31,65-101` | Reuse the separate-record-by-manifest pattern and declared-principal verification where appropriate. Its exact key schema is not the caller-site or oracle schema. Inventory discovery/fetch/retention paths for the new record explicitly. |\n| Code index / republishing: `yin/repl/ast_index.cljc:3-24,108`; `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:225-300` | Query views can be rebuilt from rows but cannot recover omitted admission provenance. Session/index-to-module publication must preserve or explicitly remap occurrence roots, and retain original evidence sources. |\n| Oracle instrumentation/accounting: `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1786-1855,2341-2405,2432-2468` | Oracle-owned trace migration, offset retirement across the full family, both lanes, refusal pins, gate accounting and evidence regeneration per R5/R6. Audit table derivation independently per R7. |\n\n### 3.3 Persisted artifacts and publication blast radius\n\n“Side tables are additive” is the compatibility policy, not permission to skip republishing coverage evidence. **Unannotated persisted artifacts remain loadable under their supported existing code contracts.** A provenance-requiring query returns missing provenance/refuses credit; it never invents identity from names, hashes, arities, values or source lines. Old provenance may be reconstructed only when the actual admission/expansion inputs are retained, and the result is a newly verified artifact.\n\nThe implementation inventory must enumerate, regenerate where changed, and verify these families on **all four host lanes**:\n\n1. `yang.call`, Clojure `clj` and its library, TypeScript `js/jssp`, Python `py/pysp`, and PHP `php` plus its hook/runtime publications. Shared emitter anchors: `yang/core/call.cljc:184`, `yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:125-143`, TS `prelude.cljc:4168-4260`, Python `prelude.cljc:3645-3739`, PHP `prelude.cljc:5328-5411`. Include PHP's definition lists `prelude_module.cljc:386` and `prelude_p9.cljc:558`; their inclusion must not escape measurement because they are emitted through the main prelude.\n2. For each host publication: canonical row closure, semantic vector, stack/register stored images and checksums, derivation records, schema-1 manifest, pinned dependency addresses, new C1 side tables/provenance/correspondence, and discovery/index/name/signature records where used. The actual publication sequence is `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:156-209`. Side-only annotation should leave old code addresses unchanged; measure this rather than assume it. New side objects still need publication, retrieval and retention.\n3. Fresh process-local prelude caches, linked harnesses and golden addresses: TS `test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:55-78`, Python `test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:55-77`, PHP `test/yang/php/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:38-56`. Reusing a realized pre-change delay is not a republication test.\n4. Imported user modules, SCC bundles and aliases, class maps, safepointed derivatives, session/module exports, signed discovery entries and DHT fixtures. Specifically `test/resources/yang/python/dht-import-v1.edn` and its generator `dht-import-v1.generate.clj:1-20`, which says to regenerate whenever lowering/publication/prelude changes addresses. Preserve old fixtures as compatibility inputs where practical, rather than rewriting away the only legacy-load test.\n5. Oracle evidence: new versioned caller-site tables; `slice9_evidence.edn`, `slice9_verification.edn`, `slice9_declarations.edn`, `slice9_unexercised.edn`, `fixture_closure.edn`, gate markdown and captured verification/test outputs; `context.edn`, `kernel_gate.edn`, `manifest.edn` and earlier slice evidence as input/history or regeneration targets according to actual dependency. Every changed trace-bearing field/pin must be listed; blanket rewriting of historical evidence is not required.\n6. Explicitly inventory `test/yang/latte_oracle/throw_sites.edn:1-7` and `case_arms.edn:1-11`. They currently contain bare vectors of source inventory maps, with no top-level schema stamp. Their producer is `src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj:15-24,52-64`; throw-site equality checking is `latte_oracle_test.clj:679-699`. They are **read/validated unchanged** if their meaning/shape remains unchanged. If caller-site coordinates or new fields are added, version their evidence contract and update producer, validator, readers, generators and pins together. They are not automatically the R1 versioned application-site table, and source `:line` is not K.\n\nThe addendum names TypeScript `publication.cljc`, but no `src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/publication.cljc` exists in the inspected checkout. TS prelude publication is presently visible in its linked harness above; its design describes later publication work (`docs/design/yang.typescript.md:1347,1545`). Whether an in-flight seat supplies a new file, renamed path, or equivalent hook is **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides**. It remains a named mandatory inventory item, not a silent omission or an invented present file.\n\n### 3.4 Failure separation\n\nPreserve the existing failure categories: malformed canonical content fails structural validation; wrong bytes/address fails hash verification; unknown execution contract refuses load; differing lowering profile reports profile mismatch or `:unverified-derivation`; wrong recomputed output reports derivation mismatch (`vm.cljc:283-285`; `ledger.cljc:156-180`; `linker.cljc:2175-2215`). New evidence-specific failures must be distinguishable: unsupported C1/evidence version, missing provenance, bad occurrence, incomplete coverage, wrong source/adaptation hash, missing lane, ambiguous correspondence, stale declared site, or reconstruction mismatch.\n\nA corrupted required provenance artifact refuses the provenance-dependent operation even if code itself verifies. An absent optional annotation does not make old code unloadable. A signature authenticates a publisher; it does not verify a correspondence. A trusted code link is not evidence of a recomputed correspondence.\n\n## 4. R1 durable correspondence schema D1\n\n### 4.1 Additive envelope\n\nDefine a new separately addressed plain-data object. The following field names and types specify D1; angle-bracket placeholders are metavariables, not values to persist.\n\n```clojure\n{:yang.correspondence/schema 1\n :yang.correspondence/evidence <Jing address of OracleEvidence>\n :yang.correspondence/recipe <Jing address of reconstruction recipe>\n :yang.correspondence/inputs [<Jing address> ...]\n :yang.correspondence/roots [<canonical UAST root address> ...]\n :yang.correspondence/origin-bindings\n [[<ephemeral site id from OracleEvidence> <persisted occurrence key>] ...]\n :yang.correspondence/artifacts\n [{:format <existing format keyword>\n   :identity <that format's identity>\n   :storage <Jing address of retrievable artifact>\n   :derivation <Jing address of applicable derivation record>} ...]\n :yang.correspondence/metadata <Jing address of C1 frontend-metadata rows>\n :yang.correspondence/instruction-provenance\n [<Jing address of an instruction-provenance relation> ...]}\n```\n\nThe D1 key set is closed; vectors preserve declared order and contain no duplicate declarations. Empty artifact/provenance vectors are allowed for a tree-only consumer, whose claimed coverage must say tree-only. `artifacts` lists lowered images only; canonical roots are retrievable row closures listed separately. Native image identity need not equal its storage address: the publisher already distinguishes checksums and stored images (`publish.cljc:159-185,204-208`). The record's address is the ordinary Jing content address of the whole map; no self-address field.\n\n`evidence` resolves to **OracleEvidence verbatim**: same EDN data shape, schema/version, field names, vectors, site ids, source-hash value and classifications. Do not project a subset or copy its rows into a second durable site-table shape. Store the whole value through a lossless codec and prove round-trip equality; preserve the original checked-in EDN bytes separately as a source artifact when byte-level archival is needed. Jing storage addressing does not replace the oracle's own canonical-source-hash algorithm. New adoption evidence must use the oracle format as frozen; unsupported representational needs require an explicit versioned extension owned by that format, never an ad hoc competing table.\n\n`inputs` names the retrievable source/adaptation/fixture/profile inputs required by the recipe, including the inlined adapter and declarations that the oracle actually hashed. A repository commit string alone is not a fetchable reconstruction input. `roots` names the produced code trees. Expansion records and admitted source-envelope identities required by the bindings must be in the retrievable input closure. The input/source artifacts may be data blobs; they must not be represented as UAST addresses unless they actually are UAST.\n\nThe recipe is a closed versioned data description, proposed as:\n\n```clojure\n{:yang.correspondence.recipe/schema 1\n :yang.correspondence.recipe/function <qualified interpreter identifier>\n :yang.correspondence.recipe/profile <address of exact reconstruction profile>\n :yang.correspondence.recipe/input-roles [[<role keyword> <input index>] ...]\n :yang.correspondence.recipe/evidence-contract <oracle's exact schema identity>\n :yang.correspondence.recipe/coverage <address of declared coverage policy>}\n```\n\nThe recipe profile pins frontend/compiler revision, grammar/options, expansion environment and recorded origins/attempts, all code-producing transformations, source canonicalization, site-table derivation, and lowering/provenance rules. The consumer must implement that exact recipe/profile. A missing upstream canonicalization rule is not repaired by choosing a plausible hash. The exact oracle schema identity remains the dependency in §1.1.\n\n`origin-bindings` is an added relation binding evidence-local sites to actual durable occurrences; **it is not a replacement attribution table**. It preserves the oracle's ephemeral ids literally. Validate every endpoint, reconstructed tree and path. A site's mapping must be unique within the claimed compilation. Multiple replay records can name the same durable occurrence only where they explicitly replay the same admission; distinct admissions must remain distinct. An oracle run without durable expansion/source records cannot be promoted merely by changing `:ephemeral` to `:expansion`. It may remain useful oracle evidence without a durable binding. To accept a relying consumer, reproduce the actual compilation under recorded durable admissions/expansions and verify its correspondence; do not retroactively assert an unrecorded event.\n\n### 4.2 Independent reconstruction and discovery\n\nThe D1 producer is the compilation/publication composition that can retain inputs, origins and outputs. The canonical compiler and lowerer remain oblivious to attribution policy except for the side artifacts they emit. D1 is an ordinary record on a separate publication/ledger stream, discoverable by its address and its code/evidence associations. The Python site-set record is a concrete precedent for “record names manifest, manifest keeps schema” (`siteset.cljc:5-22,79-101`). The implementation must pin the actual discovery schema, signature/index projection and retention owner before acceptance; this document does not pretend the current linker automatically fetches D1.\n\nVerification proceeds in this order:\n\n1. Fetch D1, recipe, OracleEvidence and every required source/row/provenance artifact; verify their addresses and structural schemas. Reject an unsupported oracle schema before interpreting any row.\n2. Verify each code image under its own execution contract and its existing derivation chain. Report content integrity separately from code derivation.\n3. Reconstruct the source/adaptation compilation under the exact recipe, using recorded source admissions and expansion attempts; recompute final roots and eligible occurrence sets. Recompute the oracle's canonical-source-hash using its exact rule.\n4. Independently re-derive the oracle site declarations from compiled AST argument expressions. Compare with OracleEvidence verbatim under that schema's comparison rules. Do not make the producer's serialized classifications the verifier's input oracle.\n5. Reconstruct every claimed ephemeral-to-persisted binding, C1 marker set and instruction mapping. Verify exact coverage and all multiplicities, including helpers that receive no upstream credit.\n6. Execute the attribution query over separately scoped evidence/occurrence/row relations; require actual body entry in both required oracle C lanes. Reconstruct the credit set and report withdrawn witnesses and stale declarations. R7 requires re-derivation, not just re-running a query over trusted declarations (`ruling-of-record.md:48-50`).\n7. Publish a separate verification result naming D1's address, recipe/profile, verifier revision, inputs, outcomes and evidence. D1 cannot mark itself “verified”. The first additional consumer's acceptance must cite this completed result.\n\nAn implementation may share the schema decoder and canonical primitives, but a verifier must not call the producer's table derivation as its only “independent” check. The existing table generator resolves the oracle's own private derivation vars (`latte_oracle_tables.clj:15-18`); regeneration through it alone does not satisfy R7.\n\nD1 does not revise old site tables, force old module manifests to schema 2, or automatically validate the oracle's historical evidence. If actual integration requires changing a closed publication record, that specific schema revision returns to the A2 inventory. Unknown recipes refuse verification; `:trusted` is never substituted for `:verifying` (linker design `:1902-1921`).\n\n## 5. A3 adoption briefs and ordering\n\n**Scheduling:** frontend adoption starts after the in-flight cell-ref sweeps and the J2 re-run land. Refresh this inventory on that merged base before changing shared prelude/publication paths. The current sweeps, J2 and oracle continue under their existing scopes. This design does not merge, pause, or extend their tasks (signed A3/A4 at lines 21–25).\n\nEach adoption brief must cite this findings file and C1/D1 by revision. The shared occurrence finalizer/validator and provenance publication work precede or accompany frontend implementation; D1 independent verification precedes acceptance of the first frontend consumer that relies on D1. A frontend may adopt static annotation without consuming oracle attribution, but cannot call that durable correspondence acceptance.\n\n### 5.1 Mandatory host sets\n\nThese are acceptance requirements, not claims that current tests already pass. **No frontend may drop Jolt because shared code compiled on another host.** Host lanes and VM execution formats are separate axes.\n\n| Frontend | JVM | Node | Dart | Jolt |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Production Clojure | Reader/source, all production entry points, publication/load/query and trace fixtures | Same portable source/forms and paths, publication/load/query and trace fixtures | Same | Same; run current reader limitations as explicit refusals, not substitute parsers |\n| TypeScript / JS dialect | Local source parser plus portable CST lowering, bundled/linked/prelude publications and load/query | Same pinned source-derived CST through portable lowering; parserless import/load; fresh prelude publication | Same packet/import/publication gate | Same packet/import/publication gate; Jolt load/parity is required |\n| PHP ANTLR + legacy PHP | Local ANTLR source parser, portable packet paths, includes/class-map/SCC, both compiler families and publications | Same source-derived CST for ANTLR; legacy source compiler; fresh publications and imports | Same | Same packet/legacy source/publication/load gate |\n| Python | Local source parser, packet/module/import/SCC/session paths, publications | Local generated parser plus all packet/session/publication paths | Local generated parser plus all packet/session/publication paths | Native C++ Python parser plus packet/session/publication/load paths; unavailable parser is a failed required source gate with a recorded reason, not a green skip |\n\nTS local parser restriction is recorded at `docs/design/yang.typescript.md:113-116,517-518`; its packet-level execution policy at `:1325-1326`. Python's host selection is implemented in `yin/repl/frontends.cljc:71-110`; Jolt's native Python adapter describes its actual parser and refusal in `src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:1-28`. PHP's local ANTLR parser is `src/clj/yang/php/antlr/cst.clj`, while the independent portable legacy compiler remains registered at `yin/repl/frontends.cljc:69-70`. Existing Jolt applicability exclusions are explicit per-test data (`test/resources/yin/repl/jolt-applicability.edn:1-5,46-48`); they cannot be expanded to evade these new gates.\n\nOn each host run static occurrence/provenance checks for AST, semantic, stack and register artifacts where the existing format admits the fixture. Execute direct walker and all three vector paths for the portable correctness corpus. Linked paths exercise all supported linked formats and pin existing unsupported/refusal cases explicitly. For example, the inspected TS linked harness documents a walker `:undeclared-free` limitation while using three vector VMs (`test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:11-15`); this design does not silently promise that old unrelated limitation is fixed. If a missing lane prevents the required new provenance test, report adoption incomplete with the named dependency.\n\n### 5.2 Cross-host equality without collapsing admissions\n\nBuild a portable replay fixture containing the SAME source or source-derived CST, grammar/options, compiler/profile revision, canonical naming inputs, source-medium/batch/member coordinates, and expansion environment/attempt inputs. The source origin contract explicitly makes these coordinates supplied plain data (`code-as-tuples.md:405-437`; `vm.cljc:938-958`).\n\nRequire:\n\n- Identical final canonical roots and the full set of `[origin root path]` keys on JVM, Node and Dart; include Jolt in the same comparison for the mandatory portable paths.\n- Identical C1 rows, reconstructed site declarations, and per-format instruction provenance. Different formats have different PCs, but they must resolve to the same intended application K.\n- For expansion fixtures, identical expansion-record addresses under replayed identities and identical producing-record chains. Normalize neither origin nor generated names away after the fact.\n- For TS/PHP packet routes, compare compilation of the same source-derived validated CST; record the JVM parser artifact. This proves compilation equality, not nonexistent local-parser parity. Python additionally compares source parsing/export across its real local parsers, following the existing exporter conformance model (`test/yang/antlr/conformance.cljc:1-20`).\n- A separate negative fixture admits identical text twice with distinct supplied admission tokens and demands different origins. Cross-host equality is replay equality; it must not turn occurrences into content identities.\n\nHost-specific source/reader conditionals that intentionally yield different programs must have separately declared cohorts and expected refusals. They cannot explain divergence for the identical portable input cohort. Include floats, metadata, generated temporaries and duplicate trees in the portable cohort because root equality is part of the gate, not just path equality.\n\n### 5.3 Frontend-specific work packages\n\n**Clojure.** Inventory native/argument-vector/outcome compile options, `compile` vs `compile-program`, `compile-form` callers, macro expansion, `lower-unit`, `library-for`, direct walker/datoms/rows lanes, REPL single/multi-form and linked runtime publication. Show a non-oracle production fixture traversing the full pipeline, including macro-generated and helper calls. Republish `clj` and `yang.call` on each host. Oracle-specific stamping is instantiation #1 only; it does not close this brief. Anchors: `yang/clojure.cljc:2021-2034,2164-2200,2791,2839-2862,2993-3021`.\n\n**TypeScript.** Cover `lower-module-body`, `lower-unit`, `lower-units`, `lower-packet`, streaming stage, bundled and linked `js`, `yang.call`, and safepoint runtime. Track new publication/SCC/session paths from the seats when they land; do not infer their implementation from design prose. Generated member/constructor/runtime dispatch applications get their own K; source-call attribution is a separate query. Anchors: `lower.cljc:1167-1211,2262-2363`; `prelude.cljc:4168-4260`.\n\n**PHP.** Cover ANTLR and still-registered legacy compiler, include/classmap/import publication, SCC bundles/aliases, strict-types variants, prelude definition lists and P9 additions. Test by-reference/indirect calls, generators and exceptional helper paths without borrowing source-call credit. Republish `php`, dependencies and all include/import fixtures. Anchors: `php.cljc:640-685`; ANTLR `lower.cljc:1710,1812,2082,2207,3737-4041`; `publication.cljc:27-70`; `prelude_module.cljc:386`; `prelude_p9.cljc:558`.\n\n**Python.** Cover packet and module lowering, import units, SCC aliases, bundled/linked `py`, `yang.call`, `pysp`, hook staging/site-set discovery, and both fresh/primed REPL sessions. Do not conflate `:yang/site` hook sites with complete application occurrence coverage. Republish the DHT fixture and validate parserless imports on other hosts. Anchors: `lower.cljc:1111-1120,1775,2018-2373`; `repl.cljc:86-108`; `publication.cljc:51-80`; `siteset.cljc:5-31`.\n\nEach brief's acceptance bundle contains: exact producer/artifact checklist; input/commit/profile pins; C1 coverage counts and root/hash invariance; four-host results and cross-host equality report; correctness battery results; before/after budgets and measurements; legacy-load/missing-provenance refusal; and D1 verification result if it relies on correspondence. No frontend can satisfy this bundle merely by running the oracle test.\n\n## 6. Measurement and declared budgets\n\nThese are measurement requirements and design cost models, **not measured performance claims**. Signed A3 requires a budget impact per frontend, and line 29 requires static/runtime separation. This read-only design has no authority to invent benchmark results. Numeric per-corpus totals remain **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides by measuring the pinned baseline before tagged runs**.\n\n### 6.1 Counts and cost model\n\nFor each fixture/prelude/module, host, and format record:\n\n- `U`: distinct canonical rows; `O`: all occurrence-tree positions; `A`: `:application` occurrences; `D`: maximum path depth; `L`: total encoded length of application paths; separate user, helper, wrapper, prelude, macro-generated and Rule-R counts as derived diagnostic groups.\n- `P_f`: instruction count/provenance-row count for format f; report shared semantic/native mapping structures separately if used.\n- `S_static`: exact encoded bytes of C1 rows, provenance tables, retained origin records, D1 and its reconstruction inputs; distinguish fresh bytes from already retained shared content, index memory and storage/dedup savings.\n- `E`: actual callee-entry records, per-lane total bytes and maximum record size; `Q_peak`: peak retained trace records and bytes, dropped/blocked writes and drain schedule.\n- Guest instruction counts and host-side work counters; wall compile/expand/project/publish/verify/load/query time; process/heap peak and retained memory, including trace buffers, indexes and pending continuations.\n\nOccurrence counts can greatly exceed unique row counts because identical subtrees are shared; an `O(U)` annotation-size promise would be false (code-as-tuples `:393-404,636-665,947-949`). A direct marker representation costs one row per A plus encoded paths/origins: storage proportional to `A + L + coordinate bytes`; traversal work proportional to visited occurrences, not unique content. Instruction mapping adds up to `P_f` rows per claimed format with path/coordinate payload. The exact peak depends on streaming vs retaining both trees/tables and must be measured.\n\n### 6.2 Experiment separation\n\nUse the same pinned code-producing inputs and three configurations:\n\n1. **B — baseline:** existing code, trace disabled.\n2. **S — static annotation:** C1/provenance production, persistence, loading and query enabled; runtime trace disabled.\n3. **T — tagged tracing:** same S outputs plus caller propagation and body-entry recording.\n\nAlso compare the oracle's existing two-field tracing with T to isolate the extra caller-site field from the original body-entry instrumentation. Report S−B and T−S separately, and the old-trace→tagged-trace delta. If instrumentation itself changes code, keep its canonical image and compile cost separate from side-only annotation. Static C1 must change **zero canonical code bytes/addresses and zero guest instructions** for otherwise identical input; runtime tracing has no such zero-cost claim.\n\nMeasure cold and warm compile/load/query separately; use fresh publication caches for cold tests, fixed workload/repetition count and host/version, and publish distribution plus raw samples rather than an unqualified single timing. Report query indexes/build time separately from query execution. Do not call a host trace hook “zero overhead” because guest instruction count did not increase.\n\n### 6.3 Bounds published before any tagged run\n\nThe implementation preflight record must contain actual finite numbers (no “TBD”, unlimited trace, or post-run adjustment) for:\n\n| Bound | Required derivation / failure |\n|---|---|\n| Maximum input rows, occurrences, A, D and total path bytes | Derive from the pinned static corpus; reject over-bound traversal before instrumentation. Include generated/bundled prelude expansion, not only source calls. |\n| Static side bytes and retained memory | Measure S without tracing; declare maximum per artifact/module and aggregate closure. Serialization size may be computed exactly before execution. Stop/revise on exceeding the declared storage/memory bound. |\n| Maximum records E and trace capacity C | Bound accepted trace writes for the fixture, declare C in records AND bytes including worst allowed site-id/path length, and declare drain behavior. No credit from a truncated/overflowed trace. |\n| Guest instruction ceiling and added work | Bound trace instructions per event/dispatch under each executed format, plus fixed setup/drain costs. Declare a ceiling for each host/format run before T; report baseline, absolute delta and ratio. |\n| Compile/expand/publish/verify/load/query time | Declare finite absolute time limits and allowed deltas against B/S in the preflight record. Pilot B/S measurements may set these; a tagged timeout is a failed bounded run. |\n| Peak process/heap/trace/index/continuation memory | Declare finite ceilings and measurement method; if a host lacks a heap counter, use a named process-memory measure and mark comparability limits. Missing measurement is not zero usage. |\n\nFor the oracle specifically, current code uses trace capacity **1,000,000 records**, drains after halt, and allows **4,000,000 guest instructions per C lane** (`latte_oracle_test.clj:1827,1847-1854,2453-2454`). These are the inspected baseline settings, not evidence the tagged run fits. Its own note records 2,024,912 walker and 1,105,792 semantic steps for the older binding fixture at line 2454. Restate both ceilings, the new record byte bound and the occupancy argument before T. If the implementation cannot establish enough capacity under those ceilings, publish revised bounds and their justification before running tagged evidence. Never silently raise fuel to get credit, or let a full trace stream park the fixture and misclassify it as semantic failure.\n\nFor other frontends, no oracle 4M/1M setting is inherited by default. Their adoption preflight must give numeric ceilings for each required fixture/prelude/host and format. Exceeding a declared bound blocks that acceptance run; changing bounds creates a new versioned run declaration and preserves the failed run.\n\n### 6.4 Budget impact allocated per frontend\n\n| Frontend | Mandatory static budget scope | Mandatory dynamic budget scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| Clojure | Native and argument-vector/outcome roots, macro outputs, guest library, linked `clj/yang.call`, repeated REPL admissions; `A_clj + L_clj + sum(P_f)` plus retained expansion/input closure | Nested/multi-arity/indirect/helper/tail calls in both production and oracle-specific fixtures; oracle owns its existing ceilings |\n| TypeScript | User units plus bundled and separately published `js/yang.call/jssp`; source→packet and packet→UAST timing separately; include post-seat publication additions | Constructor/member/indirect dispatch, exception helpers and admitted suspension paths; prelude/helper volume counted |\n| PHP | Both compiler families, includes/class-map/SCC, complete `php` including module/P9 definitions and shared runtime; peak publication closure | By-reference and indirect dispatch, include/autoload helpers, generator and exception paths |\n| Python | Fresh/primed session roots, imports/SCC, `py/yang.call/pysp`, site-set staging and DHT publication closure | Indirect descriptors, generated call dispatch, generators and hook/stream suspension paths |\n\nThe storage/work formulas are the design's budget impact; numbers require the pinned post-sweep corpus and are not asserted here. Shared `yang.call` must be reported once as retained shared content and separately as each frontend's dependency, avoiding both double charging and disappearance. Prelude republishing on four hosts is mandatory even when code hashes remain unchanged: the new occurrence/provenance artifacts need independent production/load evidence.\n\n## 7. Correctness and refusal gates\n\nEvery gate compares trace-disabled program outcomes first, then occurrence/provenance correctness, then any attribution. A marker is not proof of execution. These extend the signed oracle battery; they do not replace its seven cases and stale-table diagnostic (`ruling-of-record.md:23-34,64-69`).\n\n| Gate | Required witness and refusal |\n|---|---|\n| Duplicate content / admission | Two identical applications at different paths; identical roots at two batch indices; identical source admitted twice; identical expansion outputs from two attempts. Distinct K where required, shared content allowed. Tamper one origin/root/path and refuse cross-borrowed evidence. |\n| Macro generation | A call introduced only by expansion, nested expansions, repeated expansion attempts, final tail/stand-in transformations, and unexpanded source retained in the same compilation. Reconstruct real producing records; raw-source site counts cannot pass. |\n| Operand/operator nesting | `f(g(), h())` and an operator expression that itself calls a function. Each entered body records the caller application's K; outer pending K survives inner evaluation. If operand evaluation fails, no outer callee entry is invented. |\n| Helper/adapter delegation | A source-originating call and its adapter/helper/self-delegation use distinct Ks. Oracle `/3` self-delegation is unmapped/no credit; helper executions never donate credit to an unexecuted source site. |\n| Indirect application | Multiple runtime callees from one site and one callee from multiple sites. Static site exists independently of callee resolution. Unlinked indirect evidence refuses by default; resolve linkage without requiring a literal callee name. |\n| Tail calls and recursion | Direct/mutual tail calls, non-tail recursion, generated trampolines/adapters and hot/cold dispatch. Frame reuse must not retain the previous caller's K; repeated K is allowed, stack growth need not be introduced. |\n| Suspension/resumption | Park during operator evaluation, operand evaluation, before body entry, and inside the callee; ready-queue interleaving of two callers; resume/continuation re-entry. No global last-call variable; capture/restore caller context with the correct continuation. Resuming a body is not automatically a new body-entry event. |\n| Serialized continuation / migration | Strip ephemeral map-node stamps at canonical/continuation projection as §2.6 requires. If a consuming trace mode promises post-resume attribution, verify the separately carried durable context and its source artifact on restore. Missing context refuses attribution; do not claim migration support by retaining forbidden runtime keys. |\n| Reconstructed/shared/linked code | Load identical code under two origins; reconstruct shared row nodes; load from published preludes and another host's artifacts. Caller K is selected using the invocation's origin, never a node-id or bare segment/PC cache. |\n| Transform/staging | Safepoint insertion, module/SCC wrapping, imported code, alias projection and name canonicalization cannot leave stale roots/paths. Prove correspondence or refuse; annotate new helpers independently. |\n| Oracle disjointness | Explicit `/2` with empty value does not credit `/1`; unexecuted ambient beside executed explicit cannot borrow; wrong adapter canonical-source-hash and missing C lane refuse; declared ambient with non-ambient compiled argument fails; indirect unlinkage refuses; variable/derived context shape defaults to refusal or `/2` only. |\n| Stale table and schema | A declared id never observed in a trace produces the recorded diagnostic; unsupported versions, fabricated expansions, extra/conflicting/incomplete marker rows, missing retained inputs and wrong profiles produce distinct failures. Do not silently treat malformed evidence as an honest unexecuted site. |\n| Canonical invariance / old artifacts | Adding/removing static C1 metadata leaves canonical rows, roots, instruction vectors and executable outcomes unchanged. Supported unannotated artifacts still load; C1-dependent credit refuses as missing. |\n| Trace completeness | Buffer exhaustion, loss, interrupted run, missing trace lane or partial drains never become positive attribution. Trace-writing infrastructure must not recursively instrument its own bookkeeping into an unbounded trace. |\n\nThe relevant runtime paths are real, not hypothetical surface names: walker `apply-function` and operand frames (`ast_walker.cljc:273-332`), park/resume and ready queue (`:214-231,467-473,625-711`), semantic restoration (`semantic.cljc:102-130`), and native `:call/:ffi-call/:resume` lowering (`semantic/derive.cljc:92-102`). Frontend-specific suspension fixtures must use supported operations; unsupported language constructs remain explicit refusals. Continuation replay can produce repeated execution events, so the three-field trace is deliberately insufficient for invocation causality; D1 must not claim more than the oracle's existence-based attribution.\n\n## 8. Acceptance sequence and owner risks\n\nThe design deliverable selects C1 and D1, enumerates the known integration surfaces, and gives each frontend a bounded adoption brief. Implementation remains outside this round.\n\nAcceptance sequence:\n\n1. Oracle owns and records its versioned evidence format, origin spelling and exact source-hash/argument-shape derivation. Resolve §1.1's absent schema pin; import it verbatim into the D1 implementation.\n2. After the cell-ref sweeps/J2 land, refresh the exact producer, validator, artifact and host inventory; identify every changed contract, every read-only dependency, and any required format revision. Resolve final expansion-root ownership and native provenance completeness.\n3. Implement common occurrence/side-table production, preservation, verification and publication; retain old artifact loading. Declare numeric run bounds from the pinned baseline before tagged runs.\n4. Implement and independently reconstruct D1, including the oracle's actual tables and source/adaptation linkage. Publish verification evidence before accepting the first relying consumer.\n5. Accept each production frontend only after its complete four-host brief, cross-host equality, measurements and correctness/refusal battery pass. Preserve any unsupported lane or withdrawn witness as a failure/dependency, not an unreported skip.\n\nRisks requiring the owner's attention:\n\n- **One format, not two:** no stable caller-site evidence schema was found in the inspected oracle worktree. D1's payload contract is therefore deliberately bound by reference, with an explicit schema-freeze landing prerequisite. This design does not claim verbatim field-level integration has already been verified.\n- **Real origin ownership is unfinished:** final macro output, later safepoint transforms, SCC assembly and repeated unit admissions can change roots or reuse tokens. Portable identities need actual retained records; shape-valid fake origins would defeat the ruling.\n- **Preservation is not currently end-to-end:** encoder replacement/empty-table paths, safepoint clearing, publication's discarded provenance and content-shared reconstruction are concrete integration hazards. Native and continuation paths need their own reconstruction evidence.\n- **Prelude/host budget is unmeasured:** four-host republication is the broadest artifact obligation. No numeric annotation/trace overhead or passing lane is claimed by this design; preflight must record actual per-frontend bounds.\n- **Live-source drift:** the workspace advanced during inspection and the named TypeScript publication file is absent here. The post-sweep inventory must reconcile source, current contracts, new files and citations before implementation acceptance. The signed v3 “BEFORE acceptance” requirement governs throughout.\n\nArchitect verdict: **proceed with implementation/adoption briefs using this design, subject to the explicit inventory and evidence-schema landing prerequisites above; do not grant implementation or frontend acceptance from this document alone.** No canonical AST slot or blanket execution/module-schema bump is proposed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md && cat collab/yang-clojure/1794390804000-cc3-bounded-run-declaration-v2.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01;\n# for a fix branch, the previously-failing tests green IS the merge bar\n# (owner ruling 2026-11-07; see Gate Ladder G0).\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\n\n```sh\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\n```\n\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\n\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\n\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\n\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\n\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\n\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\n\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\n\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\n\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\n\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\n\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\n\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>[/<test-name>]` (owner ruling\n   2026-11-07: the focus rule runs the failing TEST, not the namespace —\n   `NS/test-name` entries become flutter `--plain-name` filters so only the\n   named deftests execute; fall back to bare `<ns>` only when the failing\n   var inside the namespace is not yet known, and narrow as soon as it is).\n   Fix against the failing test alone, re-run it green, then climb back up\n   (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\n   Dart bug-chases may set `DATOM_FAIL_FAST=1` (owner ruling 2026-11-07): the\n   scoped run stops at the first failure so the report arrives in minutes instead\n   of after the full scoped set. Gate runs never set it — the merge bar needs the\n   complete pass/fail picture. All dart runs use `--reporter expanded`, which\n   streams failure names and positions inline so an interrupted run still retains\n   them (the nightly's compact reporter lost 7 failure names to its 30000s wall,\n   2026-11-06).\n   Merge bar for a fix branch (owner ruling 2026-11-07, sharpening the 2026-11-01\n   ruling): when the work is fixing already-failing tests, the previously-failing\n   tests re-run green IS the merge bar — a fix branch merges on that alone; the\n   G1/G2 climb applies to new feature slices, not to fixes. The full suite still\n   gets its end-to-end confirmation from the daily nightly (04:00 VN), which is\n   what catches collateral damage, not a pre-merge suite run.\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \"Cadence\" below), so a\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-05):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\n  including the slow tier, runs **once a day at 04:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** as a\n  disjoint lane pair (`src/dev/nightly.clj`): **jupiter takes the jvm lane, shiva\n  takes node and dart** (each lane in its own clone; shiva publishes the run pin,\n  jupiter reads it and falls back to its own current master as an advisory run).\n  The measured long set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be\n  a per-merge cost; a break is instead caught by the next scheduled run.\n  The schedule is an **hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard** in\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on each host (the guard fires only at the 04 VN hour and\n  `flock` skips a slot whose previous run is still active), so it is immune to the\n  DST shifts of either host's local timezone. **The cron entries are currently\n  REMOVED (owner instruction 2026-11-06)**: the wrappers remain installed on both\n  hosts and re-arming is one crontab line each\n  (`0 * * * * ~/ci/nightly-cron.sh >> ~/ci/nightly-cron.log 2>&1`). Logs:\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.log`; results: `~/ci/nightly-results/<stamp>/summary.txt`.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). mise was moved off the apt package to the official user-local install (`~/.local/bin/mise`, 2026.11.2, owner action 2026-11-07) so both hosts share shiva's layout; toolchains in `~/.local/share/mise/installs/` were untouched. Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Kill remote processes by exact PID, never by name pattern\n\nOn these hosts, kill a runaway nightly/lane process by its exact PID\n(`pgrep -af <pattern>` only to LIST, then `kill <pid>`), never with\n`pkill -f <string>` / `killall <name>`. Measured 2026-11-06: `pkill -f\n\"nightly.clj\"` over SSH matched the invoking bash command line itself\n(the pattern appears in `ps` output of the session carrying the kill),\nterminating the SSH session mid-command — exit 255 with the kill\nhalf-done. A name-pattern kill also races concurrent seats: another\norchestrator's delegate process whose arguments embed the same string\ndies with yours. If the PID from `pgrep -af` is ambiguous (several\ncandidates), inspect with `ps -o pid,etime,args -p <pid>` first and kill\nthe exact process; a session that dies under you is a symptom of this\nmistake, not a host outage.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\n\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\ntree that did not contain them.\n\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\n\n```sh\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\ngit fetch -q origin \"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\"\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\n```\n\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \"$URL\"`). If\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\n\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\n\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\nfirst lane:\n\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\n   only (\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\n   (2026-11-02). The lane-runner fix landed 2026-11-03 (eb27fd47).\n\n### Merge verification: run the suite from a fresh clone, not the authoring worktree\n\nA merge whose new code reads a file that was never `git add`ed passes in\nthe authoring worktree (the untracked file is still on disk there) and\nbreaks everywhere else. Measured: the H3-0 landing (1fd7236b) merged\n`kernel-declarations` — which reads\n`yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn` via `io/resource` at load —\nwithout the file (untracked in the authoring worktree); master's\nyang.latte-oracle-test died at load until the file was restored\n(2026-11-04). Before landing any merge that adds code reading a new\nresource, or after any merge at all when in doubt:\n\n```sh\ngit worktree add /tmp/merge-verify <merge-commit>\ncd /tmp/merge-verify && <the touched subsystem's focused test>\ngit worktree remove /tmp/merge-verify\n```\n\nA fresh clone/worktree has only committed files — a missing untracked\nresource fails there immediately.\n\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\n\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\nchange to a ledger file:\n\n```sh\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\n```\n\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\nstaging.\n\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\n\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\n5-6-hour run.\n\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\n\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\n\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \"a second UDP bind on\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \"127.0.0.1\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\n\nOwner, verbatim: \"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\nchanges\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \"it should not be for\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\"\n\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\n\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on\n  shiva and jupiter, hourly cron plus a 04:00 Vietnam guard and flock,\n  documented under \"Cadence\" above; the driver fetches and checks out\n  origin/master before anything runs). Every lane, across the host pair:\n  the JVM full suite on jupiter, the full Node and Dart suites on shiva.\n  Its results are a\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\n\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\nnightly owns the follow-up.\n\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\npre-existing by the owning seat.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\n\"free laptop\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\n\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\n\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\n**Dart quick tier\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\n\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\n\"Patch does not apply\" or \"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\".\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\n\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\n\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \"run tests remotely\nover SSH\" to \"delegate whole work agents remotely\": a heavy implementation or\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\n\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\nreference):\n\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\n   prepare the scratch clone per \"Get the code there\".\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \"$(mise env -s\n   bash)\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\n   inside the same command.\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\n\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 13:40:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 20:40:00 +0700\nCoding-Agent: muse\nSession-ID: 7db9b5cc-3472-4c31-9657-1b41aaa76ae3\n\n# cc3 bounded run declaration v2 — NEW versioned run (astra r4 ruling (c))\n\nThis declaration is written BEFORE the v2 measurement run, per design\n§6.3 and astra's r4 exceedance ruling (c). It creates a NEW, explicitly\nversioned bounded run. It does not amend, re-scope, or relabel the\nsuperseded run (see §5).\n\n## 1. Pinned corpus, revision, host, and JVM settings\n\n| Item | Pinned value |\n|---|---|\n| Worktree / branch | `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3`, branch `cc3-lowering-provenance` |\n| Base revision | `ced9f2c2bdb43ab4a416b4ab408c76eb29245764` plus the uncommitted cc3 diff (ten tracked files, +1651/−98 at r6 delivery: the eight cc3 files plus the two F7 diagnostic scripts `test/d2_wedge_min_repro.clj` / `test/d2_wedge_probe2.clj`, which the measurement script does not load; latte oracle test file further changed in r5 for F2 caller threading — behaviour-neutral for this fixture's compiled content). Exact pin: see the digest rows below and §6. |\n| Fixture (the whole corpus of this run) | `{:id :cc3-binding :call '(latte-kernel.utils/vectorn? [1 2 3] 2)}` admitted through `kernel-compile`, bound through `cc3-oracle-binding` |\n| Upstream oracle revision | latte-kernel `86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279` (pinned in `deps.edn` `:test` alias) |\n| Compiler base recorded by the evidence harness | `7dc18478` |\n| Host | Darwin 25.6.0 arm64, Apple M1 Pro, 17,179,869,184 B RAM |\n| JVM | OpenJDK 17.0.18+0 (Homebrew, 64-Bit Server VM, mixed mode) |\n| Clojure | Clojure CLI 1.12.4.1602; no explicit `-Xmx` (JVM default max heap). JVM stack: **corrected in r6 (V2)** — the v2 (r5) measured command (§4 as originally written) activated ONLY the `:f6` alias, which supplied `:extra-paths [\"test\"]` and the latte-kernel `:extra-deps` and NO `:jvm-opts`; the project `:test` alias was NOT activated, so its `-Xss64m` (`deps.edn:52`) and its test-runner main-opts were NOT in effect for the v2 run. The v2 samples therefore stand as measured under the JVM default thread stack. The v2.1 declared command (§4, r6 revision) supplies `-Xss64m` explicitly through `:f6`'s own `:jvm-opts [\"-Xss64m\"]`, and the v2.1 results in §6 were measured under exactly that declared command. `-Xss64m` is declared for parity with the `:test` stack policy and headroom, not because the v2 ceilings depended on it: the v2 samples already met every ceiling without it. |\n| Measurement script | `collab/yang-clojure/1794386683000-cc3-f6-measure.clj` (in-repo, r5 revision: maximum-based rule, compiled-inclusive holder) |\n| Measured patch pin (SHA-256) | `git diff` (ten tracked files listed in §6) = `05076f51add1a5b5285c62c6b85c7eb8875d7c2780a7b33613e46742a250887d` (computed 2026-11-11 after the final r6 code edit; reproduce with `git diff \\| shasum -a 256` in this worktree) |\n| Measurement script pin (SHA-256) | `shasum -a 256 collab/yang-clojure/1794386683000-cc3-f6-measure.clj` = `345b432dace388ce20bb463d2241c650b400b644b5194cd0ddae1a3fb42b37c8` |\n\n## 2. Retained object set and maximum simultaneous retention\n\nThe retained set for one fixture, all held simultaneously by one local\nholder (never a global Var), is:\n\n- the **compiled input**: the `kernel-compile` result (compiled AST,\n  stamped AST, site table, retained `:admission-origin`);\n- the **binding**: `cc3-oracle-binding` result (C1 tree, rows, marked\n  and eligible occurrence sets, site→key binding);\n- the **reconstruction evidence**: the binding's stamped tree/rows,\n  stamped marked/eligible sets, site correspondence map, and stamped\n  paths — i.e. everything needed to reconstruct and verify each bound\n  key from the retained compilation;\n- the lowered images: `linearize/project-rows` output plus\n  `derive/stack-image` and `derive/register-image`;\n- the provenance relation (schema-1 map over admission/root and the\n  semantic, stack, and register call-provenance) and, in the pipeline\n  variant, its durable record in a file-backed content store.\n\nTwo holder variants are measured: **binding-only** (binding, lowered\nimages, relation, reconstruction evidence — the compiled input is\nreleased after binding) and **compiled-inclusive** (the same set plus\nthe retained compiled input). The compiled-inclusive variant is the\nacceptance variant; the binding-only variant is reported for\ncomparison and is NOT the acceptance holder.\n\n**Maximum simultaneously retained fixtures: 1.** Every retained and\npeak measurement holds exactly one fixture's retained set at a time;\nthe previous holder is released (and GC-pinned) before the next\nrepetition builds a new one.\n\n## 3. Ceilings (separate, finite, pilot-justified)\n\nPilot measurements (superseded run, r4, warm JVM, same host/JVM):\nbinding-only retained samples 7,960,192 / 7,921,728 / 8,044,024 B\n(median 7,960,192 B, **maximum 8,044,024 B**); compiled-inclusive\nsamples 9,008,912 / 8,939,024 / 8,826,416 B (median 8,939,024 B,\n**maximum 9,008,912 B**); serialized rows 293,924 B; serialized\nrelation 802,260 B; peak used heap 292,720,240 B (GC-pinned baseline\n38,967,336 B); pipeline elapsed 1,727 ms.\n\n| Ceiling | v2 bound | Justification / headroom over pilot |\n|---|---|---|\n| Storage, per artifact (serialized canonical bytes) | **2,000,000 B** | Kept from r2. Pilot rows 293,924 B (6.8× headroom), relation 802,260 B (2.5× headroom). |\n| Storage, aggregate retained serialized set (rows + relation + durable record, 1 fixture) | **4,000,000 B** | Pilot rows+relation = 1,096,184 B; the durable record is a small map over the same data. 4,000,000 B is ≥3.6× the pilot partial sum, leaving room for the record while staying finite. |\n| Retained memory, aggregate (1 fixture, compiled-inclusive holder) | **12,000,000 B** | Set ABOVE the pilot compiled-inclusive **maximum** 9,008,912 B by 2,991,088 B (**+33.2% headroom**; +34.2% over the 8,939,024 B median; +49.2% over the binding-only maximum 8,044,024 B). Pilot repetition spread was ~2% (8,826,416–9,008,912 B), so 33% covers GC-delta noise, the longer r5 run-scope strings (tens of bytes), and host jitter on this machine class, without being an order-of-magnitude loosening. The floor is the measured maximum, per ruling (c): a ceiling at or below 9,008,912 B would declare a run already known to exceed it. |\n| Peak heap (absolute used heap during the pipeline) | **1,073,741,824 B (1 GiB)** | Kept from r2. Pilot peak 292,720,240 B is 27.3% of this ceiling (3.67× headroom); the figure is absolute used heap (baseline + harness + transients), so the margin absorbs sampler/baseline variance. |\n| Time, per pipeline repetition (compile → persistence → both tagged lanes) | **10,000 ms** | Kept from r2. Pilot 1,727 ms gives 5.8× headroom; warm-JVM figure, cold-JVM limits are stated in §4. |\n| Guest instructions / trace records per C lane | **4,000,000 / 1,000,000** | Restated from the r1 record, NOT raised. |\n\n## 4. Exact reproducible measurement procedure\n\nScript: `collab/yang-clojure/1794386683000-cc3-f6-measure.clj`\n(r5 revision). Run from the worktree root:\n\n```\nclojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:f6 {:extra-paths [\"test\"] :jvm-opts [\"-Xss64m\"] :extra-deps {latte-kernel/latte-kernel {:git/url \"https://github.com/latte-central/latte-kernel\" :git/sha \"86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279\"}}}}}' -M:f6 collab/yang-clojure/1794386683000-cc3-f6-measure.clj\n```\n\n(Settings actually in effect, r6-corrected: the `:f6` alias defined\ninline by `-Sdeps` supplies everything — `:extra-paths [\"test\"]` puts\nthe oracle test namespace on the classpath, `:jvm-opts [\"-Xss64m\"]`\nsets the thread stack (added in the r6 revision of this command; the\nv2 run used the same command WITHOUT `:jvm-opts`, i.e. the JVM\ndefault stack — see §1), and `:extra-deps` pins latte-kernel. The\nproject's `:test` alias is NOT activated: its `-Xss64m`\n(`deps.edn:52`) and test-runner main opts play no part. The\nlatte-kernel git dependency is named explicitly because the `:test`\nalias, which normally carries it, also carries the test-runner main\nopts; the git checkout is already cached locally. Verdicts are read\nfrom the script's printed `:under-ceilings-v2` map, computed by the\nmaximum rule below. The v2.1 results under this exact declared\ncommand are recorded in §6.)\n\nProcedure, in order:\n\n1. **Warmup**: build one retained structure and one full pipeline\n   pass, DISCARD both, GC-pin. Class loading / JIT are charged to the\n   warmup, not to measured repetitions.\n2. **Retained memory**: 5 repetitions per holder variant\n   (binding-only, then compiled-inclusive). Each repetition builds a\n   fresh holder held only by a local, samples GC-pinned used heap\n   (`System/gc()`, 50 ms, `System/gc()`, then\n   `Runtime.totalMemory − freeMemory`) before and after, uses\n   `System/identityHashCode` on the holder AFTER the second sample so\n   it cannot be collected early, and releases it on return. ALL 5\n   samples per variant are recorded.\n3. **Peak / pipeline**: 3 pipeline repetitions. A sampler thread\n   records used heap every 2 ms across lowering → binding /\n   projection / derives → actual serialization\n   (`dao.jing/canonical-bytes`) → durable persistence\n   (`dao.jing/materialize!` of relation and record into a file-backed\n   `yin.repl.store`, closed) → tagged execution (both C lanes must\n   reach `:completed`). Peak, per-repetition elapsed time, and\n   serialized byte counts are recorded per repetition.\n4. **Pass/fail is MAXIMUM-based** (r5 F6 fix): a ceiling is met only\n   if the MAXIMUM observed value is at or under it — retained memory:\n   max of the 5 compiled-inclusive samples ≤ 12,000,000 B (the\n   binding-only samples are reported, and also max-checked against\n   the same aggregate ceiling for information); storage: max per\n   artifact and max aggregate ≤ their ceilings; peak heap: max peak\n   over the 3 repetitions ≤ 1 GiB; time: max elapsed ≤ 10,000 ms.\n   Any single sample over its ceiling = EXCEEDED. Medians are\n   printed for information only and never establish compliance.\n\nSampling limitations (named, not hidden): retained and peak figures\nare process-wide JVM used-heap values, not an object-graph census;\n`System/gc()` is a hint the JVM honored observably here, not a\nguarantee; 2 ms peak sampling can miss sub-2 ms transients; figures\nare comparable only on this host/JVM class and only for the named\nholder scope; times are warm-JVM (the discarded warmup precedes all\nmeasured repetitions).\n\n## 5. Superseded run: failed status preserved\n\nThe r2/r4 bounded run (8,000,000 B aggregate retained ceiling,\nmedian-based accounting in its r4 record) **FAILED and remains\nfailed**. Its exceedances, restated with maximum-based accounting:\n\n- compiled-inclusive holder: samples 9,008,912 / 8,939,024 /\n  8,826,416 B — every sample over the 8,000,000 B ceiling.\n  **EXCEEDED.**\n- binding-only holder: samples 7,960,192 / 7,921,728 / 8,044,024 B —\n  the 8,044,024 B sample is over the 8,000,000 B ceiling, so under\n  the corrected maximum rule this holder is **EXCEEDED** too (its r4\n  \"under (99.5%)\" median label is corrected in r5; the samples\n  themselves are unchanged).\n\nNothing in this v2 declaration relabels that run successful, raises\nits ceiling retroactively, or shrinks its fixture. The v2 run below\nis a separate, new run under the ceilings in §3; its results are\nrecorded in the r5 findings, not back-written into the superseded\nrun's record.\n\n## 6. Exact pin and v2.1 re-measurement under the corrected declared settings (r6, V2)\n\nPin (computed 2026-11-11 after the final r6 code edit; nothing in\nthe measured patch changed afterwards — the digest was re-verified\nidentical after the diagnostic-script runs and lint):\n\n- Ten-file tracked diff (`git diff`, +1651/−98):\n  `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc`,\n  `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc`,\n  `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc`,\n  `test/d2_wedge_min_repro.clj`, `test/d2_wedge_probe2.clj`,\n  `test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc`,\n  `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`,\n  `test/yin/vm/semantic/derive_test.cljc`,\n  `test/yin/vm/semantic/linearize_test.cljc`.\n  SHA-256 of `git diff` output:\n  `05076f51add1a5b5285c62c6b85c7eb8875d7c2780a7b33613e46742a250887d`.\n- Measurement script SHA-256:\n  `345b432dace388ce20bb463d2241c650b400b644b5194cd0ddae1a3fb42b37c8`.\n- This declaration and the findings are untracked collab files and\n  are not part of the `git diff` digest. Nothing is committed.\n\nV2.1 run: the §4 command exactly as now declared (with\n`:jvm-opts [\"-Xss64m\"]` supplied by `:f6`), 2026-11-11, warm JVM,\nthis host/JVM; run scope\n`cc3-f6-measure-v2-326ca2ee-2f81-4711-9377-42cd2c62e080`;\nafter-release GC-used heap 39,226,944 B; both C lanes `:completed`\nin all 3 pipeline repetitions; the run's `/tmp/cc3-f6-store-*` file\nstores were removed afterwards. All samples recorded; verdicts are\nmaximum-based (§4 rule):\n\n| Quantity | V2.1 samples (all recorded) | Maximum | Ceiling (§3) | Verdict (max rule) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Retained: compiled-inclusive holder (acceptance) | 8,828,592 / 8,832,864 / 8,888,424 / 8,829,928 / 8,924,640 B | 8,924,640 B | 12,000,000 B | PASS (74.4% of ceiling) |\n| Retained: binding-only holder (informational) | 8,203,464 / 7,920,344 / 7,918,160 / 8,014,944 / 7,923,464 B | 8,203,464 B | 12,000,000 B | under (informational; not the acceptance holder) |\n| Storage: rows canonical bytes | 312,886 B ×3 | 312,886 B | 2,000,000 B | PASS |\n| Storage: relation canonical bytes | 856,315 B ×3 | 856,315 B | 2,000,000 B | PASS |\n| Storage: durable record canonical bytes | 544 B ×3 | 544 B | — (in aggregate) | — |\n| Storage: aggregate retained set | 1,169,745 B ×3 | 1,169,745 B | 4,000,000 B | PASS |\n| Peak used heap (2 ms sampling) | 251,948,768 / 272,026,880 / 263,341,656 B | 272,026,880 B | 1,073,741,824 B | PASS |\n| Pipeline elapsed | 772 / 769 / 743 ms | 772 ms | 10,000 ms | PASS |\n\nThe script's printed `:under-ceilings-v2` map was all-true. The\nv2.1 figures sit beside the v2 (r5) figures (compiled-inclusive max\n8,931,952 B without `-Xss64m`; v2.1 max 8,924,640 B with it) — the\nceiling holds under both stack settings, which is why §1 records\n`-Xss64m` as declared parity/headroom rather than a requirement.\nSerialized sizes are unchanged from v2 (the measurement fixture's\nadmission scope is the script's own run scope in both runs).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
