r-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md\\nrg -n --glob '*.md' 'P1-a|P1-b|DO NOT LAND|deterministic default|identical generated' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure\\nrg --files /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure | rg '(architect.*(cc1|recheck|fix|review)|17952|17950)'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-08 2x:xx +0700\\n     2\\tCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode, yang.clojure orchestrator seat)\\n     3\\tStatus: DRAFT — pending owner sign-off (ruling text from the owner's words in session; filed verbatim-in-substance below)\\n     4\\t\\n     5\\t# OWNER RULING OF RECORD — occurrence identity under unitarity (resolves the codex cc1 gate reject)\\n     6\\t\\n     7\\t## The owner's principle (quoted-in-substance)\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\tThe compilation pipeline is transformation of values over dao.stream, so unitarity applies: **information is never lost**. The mapping is **bijective** — two things may compile to the same thing and should have the same content hash, but because of unitarity, **the complete history of the transformation is intact**. Two things may compile to the same thing and have the same hash **but have different history that can be recovered**.\\n    10\\t\\n    11\\t## Rulings derived (U1–U3)\\n    12\\t\\n    13\\t**U1 — an occurrence is not its content address.** The occurrence identity is **(content address, origin)** — the address as reached through a specific transformation event. \\\"Two occurrences of identical content\\\" is well-formed: they differ in history, not content. The per-occurrence origins in the call-site side table, keyed on `[origin root-address path]`, ARE the query that recovers the distinction (design §2.1/§2.3). Codex's P1-a objection (\\\"identical generated/input applications share an address, so provenance cannot be classified by content\\\") is ANSWERED: provenance is not classified by content — it is read from the retained history: the expansion-event record behind a `[:expansion record-address]` origin, the source position behind a `[:source …]` origin. Never minted, never guessed from content.\\n    14\\t\\n    15\\t**U2 — admission identity is history-recoverable, declared by the composition.** If the composition's history contains two admission events, bijectivity demands both are recoverable — collapsing them into one deterministic token loses information and violates unitarity. If the history contains one event, minting a fresh token fabricates history — the violation in the other direction. Only the composition knows which its history contains, so the composition DECLARES the distinction when it exists (the caller-owned admission scope / explicit `:yin.link.publish/admission` token), and the deterministic content-derived default is CORRECT precisely because re-publication of identical content with no declared distinction IS one event. Codex's P1-b objection is ANSWERED on these grounds: the requirement is not \\\"every admission mints a fresh token\\\" (that fabricates history) but \\\"every DISTINCT event is representable and recoverable\\\" (the scoped/explicit mechanism provides it; the composition supplies it when its history contains two events).\\n    16\\t\\n    17\\t**U3 — the general principle.** Identity is never minted from structure OR from process. Occurrence distinctness is either (i) READ from retained history (per-occurrence origins, expansion events), or (ii) DECLARED by the party whose history it is (composition-supplied admission identities). Provenance remains recoverable by address or query over datoms — axiom 2 holds in both channels.\\n    18\\t\\n    19\\t## Consequences\\n    20\\t\\n    21\\t- The cc1 branch state (d91489f7) STANDS as the implementation of U1/U2 — no further design-level code change is required to answer the codex reject; the objection is resolved on the record by this ruling.\\n    22\\t- The landing sequence proceeds: astra re-check against this ruling → codex re-gate → merge to master → the exception track's R3 dispatch → adoption rounds 2–4 + D1.\\n    23\\t- Addendum A's occurrence contract is unaffected in shape: K = [origin root-address path], `:yang/call-site 1`, the occurrence/content boundary — all unchanged. U1 clarifies WHAT an origin identifies (an event in the retained history), not how it attaches.\\n    24\\t\\n    25\\t— drafted by the yang.clojure orchestrator seat from the owner's ruling in session\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794230000000-engineer-cljrt-fix-round.prompt.md:12:Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt`, branch `yang-clojure-runtime-module`, HEAD `e267bcf3` (your own runtime-module commit — published module, clj/run-main, globals, the :clojure profile, both matrix legs; seat-verified matrix 9/11/0 and yang.base 122/0). The codex quality gate says DO NOT LAND YET — three correctness defects, full verdict in `collab/yang-clojure/1794230000000-reviewer-cljrt.codex.stdout.log` (read it first; the block beginning \\\"Verdict: do not land yet\\\").\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md:13:**U1 — an occurrence is not its content address.** The occurrence identity is **(content address, origin)** — the address as reached through a specific transformation event. \\\"Two occurrences of identical content\\\" is well-formed: they differ in history, not content. The per-occurrence origins in the call-site side table, keyed on `[origin root-address path]`, ARE the query that recovers the distinction (design §2.1/§2.3). Codex's P1-a objection (\\\"identical generated/input applications share an address, so provenance cannot be classified by content\\\") is ANSWERED: provenance is not classified by content — it is read from the retained history: the expansion-event record behind a `[:expansion record-address]` origin, the source position behind a `[:source …]` origin. Never minted, never guessed from content.\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md:15:**U2 — admission identity is history-recoverable, declared by the composition.** If the composition's history contains two admission events, bijectivity demands both are recoverable — collapsing them into one deterministic token loses information and violates unitarity. If the history contains one event, minting a fresh token fabricates history — the violation in the other direction. Only the composition knows which its history contains, so the composition DECLARES the distinction when it exists (the caller-owned admission scope / explicit `:yin.link.publish/admission` token), and the deterministic content-derived default is CORRECT precisely because re-publication of identical content with no declared distinction IS one event. Codex's P1-b objection is ANSWERED on these grounds: the requirement is not \\\"every admission mints a fresh token\\\" (that fabricates history) but \\\"every DISTINCT event is representable and recoverable\\\" (the scoped/explicit mechanism provides it; the composition supplies it when its history contains two events).\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794065655000-architect-stream-eval-summary-fix-adjudication.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795050000000-architect-attribution-as-query-fix-review.diff\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793954848000-architect-jolt-reader-conditional-review.prompt.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795010000000-reviewer-cc1-fix.codex.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793954848000-architect-jolt-reader-conditional-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794065655000-architect-stream-eval-summary-fix-adjudication.prompt.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793954848000-architect-jolt-reader-conditional-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795060000000-architect-p1-recheck.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795020000000-reviewer-cc1-fix2.codex.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794620000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.kimi-review.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795040000000-engineer-exception-r3.prompt.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793950384477-architect-dart-cljd-guards-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794065411000-architect-stream-eval-summary-fix-adjudication.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793850000000-architect-h3-1-packet-review.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795060000000-architect-p1-amendments.diff\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795050000000-architect-attribution-fix-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793950384477-architect-dart-cljd-guards-review.prompt.md\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795220000000-reviewer-cc1-fix4.codex.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795315000000-architect-unitarity-ruling-recheck.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793950384477-architect-dart-cljd-guards-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794421000000-reviewer-cc3-r11-confirm-jupiter-r5.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg --files -uuu collab/yang-clojure | rg '(fix-r(7|8|9|10|11)|r(7|8|9|10)-confirm)'; sed -n '1280,1388p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; sed -n '1650,1830p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; sed -n '2390,2555p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; git diff ced9f2c2..897786cf -- src/cljc/yin/vm\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\n\\n(defn- finalize-eval\\n  \\\"Drain the round's output and its result from the output medium.  Only a\\n   result stamped with this round's identity is this round's value; the\\n   evaluator answers once per halted program, after its prints.  The\\n   render hook and the default result formatting are host code\\n   executing at the round's very end; a raise from either reaches the\\n   round recovery, which reads retention evidence only from the\\n   shell-owned accumulator, never from the raise payload (r11).  A missing\\n   or foreign-round result is loss, reported with the append outcome the\\n   runner recorded.  The program halted, so its VM state survives and a\\n   frontend's staged session is committed; a frontend with a `:render`\\n   shows its value as that renders it, the loop printing for every language.\\\"\\n  [state state' vm']\\n  (let [append (::result-append vm')\\n        render (:render (::frontend state'))\\n        [state'' output-text results]\\n        (drain-output (commit-frontend\\n                        (assoc state' :vm (dissoc vm' ::result-append))))\\n        round (round-id state')\\n        current (filterv #(= round (:round %)) results)]\\n    (if (seq current)\\n      (let [value (:value (peek current))]\\n        [(record-last-value state state'' value)\\n         (str output-text\\n              (if render\\n                (render value)\\n                (format-value value (host-fn-namer (:vm state'')))))])\\n      [state''\\n       (str output-text \\\"Error: \\\" result-loss-text\\n            (when (and append (not= :dao.stream/ok append))\\n              (str \\\" (append: \\\" (name append) \\\")\\\")))])))\\n\\n\\n(defn- drain-observer\\n  \\\"Advance one observer past every batch still on its medium, keeping any\\n   gap it recovers across.  A nil observer — a session without that medium —\\n   stays nil.\\\"\\n  [observer]\\n  (when observer\\n    (loop [observer observer]\\n      (let [{:keys [status] observer' :observer}\\n            (observer/observe-next observer)]\\n        (if (#{:ok :gap} status) (recur observer') observer')))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- ingress-gaps\\n  \\\"Gaps the evaluation path's observers counted.  The code indexer's are\\n   its own: an index loss is reported without refusing evaluation.\\\"\\n  [state]\\n  (+ (:ingress-gaps (:observer state) 0)\\n     (:ingress-gaps (:row-observer state) 0)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- consume-failed-round\\n  \\\"Answer a round that threw with a recoverable shell state.\\n\\n   The failed input is consumed exactly once: each of the session's\\n   observers resumes from the session the throw carried — the failing\\n   stage's, matched by the medium it holds — or from where the round began\\n   when it carried none, and reads past every batch still on its medium.\\n   The shell is the only appender and appends one batch per round, so what\\n   remains is the failed batch alone.  The VM is the one the round began\\n   with, so a half-run continuation cannot be resumed — and its error\\n   replayed — by the next input.  Output the round already emitted is\\n   drained here, once.\\\"\\n  [state error]\\n  (let [carried (get-in (ex-data error) [:session :observer])\\n        resume (fn [observer]\\n                 (if (and observer\\n                          carried\\n                          (= (:stream carried) (:stream observer)))\\n                   carried\\n                   observer))\\n        drained (assoc state\\n                       :observer (drain-observer (resume (:observer state)))\\n                       :row-observer (drain-observer\\n                                       (resume (:row-observer state)))\\n                       :indexer (index/skip (:indexer state))\\n                       :ast-indexer (ast-index/skip (:ast-indexer state)))\\n        [state' output-text]\\n        (drain-output\\n          (cond-> drained\\n            (> (ingress-gaps drained) (ingress-gaps state))\\n            (assoc :ingress-loss? true)))]\\n    [state' (str output-text (format-error error))]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- run-index-stage\\n  \\\"Drive the code indexer's observer over `program-out`: it commits each\\n   forwarded packet as one transaction and publishes the covered indexes.\\n   It runs before the evaluator, so a program is indexed whether its VM\\n   returns, parks, or raises; the two observers meet only at the medium.\\\"\\n  [state]\\n  (update state :indexer index/step (:round state)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- run-ast-index-stage\\n  \\\"Drive the AST indexer's observer over `program-out`: it adds each\\n   forwarded packet's rows and occurrences to the session's `$ast` and\\n   `$occ` relations.  It runs before the evaluator for the same reason the\\n   code indexer does, and neither indexer reads the other.\\\"\\n  [state]\\n  (update state :ast-indexer ast-index/step))\\n\\n\\n(defn- with-index-notice\\n  \\\"Append what the round's index steps lost, if anything, to its result\\n   counters never advanced -- must not let a later require or install\\n   mint an identity the pair has already answered.\\\"\\n  [base used]\\n  (let [n (max (or (:id-counter base) 0) (or (:id-counter used) 0))\\n        o (max (or (:origins base) 0) (or (:origins used) 0))]\\n    (cond-> base\\n      (pos? n) (assoc :id-counter n)\\n      (pos? o) (assoc :origins o))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- link-raise\\n  \\\"The raise of a resumed link wait, carrying the identity the parked\\n   evaluation has minted so far -- the counters of the VM the raise\\n   interrupted, whose every earlier run is in them -- so a rollback\\n   cannot reuse it.  It carries the query interpreter's pair too: the\\n   requests it has answered stay answered when the shell rolls back.\\n   The linker successor carries every cursor and settled question.\\n   It carries no retention evidence (r11): the handles and failures\\n   completed retention persisted are on the session state the\\n   recovery reads -- a program that returned from the evaluator stage\\n   waiting on a query/link has its handles on `state`, and a throw\\n   when `drive-links` resumes it drops nothing -- so nothing a guest\\n   or host throw carries is retention evidence.\\\"\\n  [error vm state]\\n  (ex-info (or (ex-message error) \\\"Link failed\\\")\\n           (assoc (or (ex-data error) {})\\n                  ::link-counter (:id-counter vm)\\n                  ::link-origins (:origins vm)\\n                  ::query-pair (:query-pair state)\\n                  ::linker (:linker state)\\n                  ::dht (:dht state)\\n                  ::indexer (:indexer state))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- carry-served\\n  \\\"`state` rolled back by a link raise, keeping what the interpreters\\n   already concluded: the query pair, the link pair past every request\\n   answered, the linker interpreter's past every\\n   question settled, the DHT node a load started or forgot on, and the\\n   indexer a `yin.link/publish` committed its name envelopes through.\\\"\\n  [state d]\\n  (cond-> state\\n    (::query-pair d) (assoc :query-pair (::query-pair d))\\n    (::linker d) (assoc :linker (::linker d))\\n    (contains? d ::dht) (assoc :dht (::dht d))\\n    (::indexer d) (assoc :indexer (::indexer d))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- link-context\\n  \\\"What a `yin.link` call is answered under (yin.repl.query/serve): the\\n   reader's authority, the publisher's key, and the requesting VM's\\n   primitives and module registry -- the modules it linked are derived\\n   from it, never recorded -- and the round.\\\"\\n  [state vm]\\n  {:authority (link/authority (:link-source state))\\n   :key (:dht-key state)\\n   :primitives (:primitives vm)\\n   :modules (:modules vm)\\n   :call-site-tables (call-site-tables state)\\n   :call-site-admissions (call-site-admissions state)\\n   :round (:round state)})\\n\\n\\n(defn- drive-links\\n  \\\"Serve the linker interpreter and run the parked VM alternately, at most\\n   `link-round-budget` rounds, while `vm` waits on a link.  A round that\\n   moves nothing ends the drive: a link whose content source cannot\\n   answer will not answer by waiting longer.  Returns\\n   `[state vm pending progress?]`, `pending` the links the interpreter\\n   reported `:pending`, and `progress?` true when a round moved\\n   something -- advanced a serve cursor, delivered a response or\\n   completed an install -- the fact the pending run's `:checks` resets\\n   on (yin.repl.link-policy.md section 3.1).  A refused or lost link\\n   raises as the VM's own, carrying the interrupted VM's minted\\n   identity.\\n\\n   `initial-progress` records progress from the caller's round.  When\\n   true, the first iteration runs the VM once without a round of its own.\\n\\n   A VM waiting on a `dao.space.query/q` call is driven the same way: the\\n   query interpreter (`yin.repl.query/serve`) answers from the session's\\n   indexer and the VM runs on.  Only a round the VM spends waiting on a\\n   link counts against `link-round-budget`; a query is answered in the\\n   round it is asked, so its rounds are bounded by `query-drive-budget`\\n   calls instead: a VM still waiting on a call once the drive has\\n   answered that many raises `query-call-limit-text` as its own error,\\n   the unanswered request abandoned with it.\\\"\\n  [state vm initial-progress]\\n  (loop [i 0\\n         calls 0\\n         vm vm\\n         state state\\n         pending []\\n         progress? initial-progress\\n         initial? initial-progress]\\n    (let [link? (link-waiting? vm)\\n          query? (query/waiting? vm)]\\n      (cond\\n        (or (vm/halted? vm) (not (or link? query?)) (>= i link-round-budget))\\n        [state vm pending progress?]\\n\\n        (and query? (>= calls query-drive-budget))\\n        (throw (link-raise (ex-info query-call-limit-text\\n                                    {:reason :yin.repl.query/call-limit\\n                                     :limit query-drive-budget})\\n                           vm\\n                           (update state :query-pair query/abandon-requests)))\\n\\n        :else\\n        (let [served (when (and link? (not initial?))\\n                       (link/round (assoc (:linker state)\\n                                          :source (:link-source state))\\n                                   (:dht state)))\\n              state (cond-> state\\n                      served (assoc :linker (:linker served)\\n                                    :dht (:dht served)))\\n              answered (when query?\\n                         (query/serve {:pair (:query-pair state)\\n                                       :indexer (:indexer state)\\n                                       :ast-indexer (:ast-indexer state)\\n                                       :dht (:dht state)\\n                                       :link (link-context state vm)\\n                                       :limits {:row-limit query-row-limit\\n                                                :byte-limit query-byte-limit}\\n                                       :budget (min query-serve-budget\\n                                                    (- query-drive-budget\\n                                                       calls))}))\\n              state (cond-> state\\n                      answered (assoc :query-pair (:pair answered)\\n                                      :dht (:dht answered)\\n                                      :indexer (:indexer answered)))\\n              pending (if (seq (:pending served)) (:pending served) pending)]\\n          (if (or initial? (:progress? served)\\n                  (:progress? answered))\\n            (let [vm' (try (vm/run vm)\\n                           (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error)\\n                                  error\\n                             (throw (link-raise error vm state))))]\\n              (recur (if link? (inc i) i)\\n                     (+ calls (:answered answered 0))\\n                     vm'\\n                     state\\n                     pending\\n                     true\\n                     false))\\n            [state vm pending progress?]))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- tokenize\\n  \\\"Answer the halted VM's value on the output medium under `state`'s\\n   round, as the evaluator runner does for a program it runs itself.  A\\n   resumed require halts outside the observer loop, so the shell emits\\n   its token here.\\\"\\n  [state vm]\\n  ((make-runner (:output-stream state) (round-id state)) vm))\\n\\n\\n(defn- pending-text\\n  [pending]\\n  (if (seq pending)\\n    (str \\\";; require pending: \\\"\\n         (str/join \\\", \\\" (map (fn [p]\\n                               (str (pr-str (:name p))\\n                                    \\\" (link \\\" (pr-str (:yin.link/id p))\\n                                    (when (:admission p)\\n                                      \\\", awaiting admission\\\")\\n                                    \\\")\\\"))\\n                             pending))\\n         \\\"); lines typed meanwhile run when it completes, (abandon) gives\\\"\\n         \\\" up\\\\n\\\")\\n    (str \\\";; require still pending; lines typed meanwhile run when it\\\"\\n         \\\" completes, (abandon) gives up\\\\n\\\")))\\n\\n\\n(defn- dropped-lines-text\\n  [n]\\n  (when (pos? n)\\n    (str \\\";; dropped \\\" n \\\" line\\\" (when (> n 1) \\\"s\\\")\\n         \\\" typed while the require was pending\\\\n\\\")))\\n\\n\\n                     \\\" (store cleared)\\\"))]\\n             [state (str \\\"Error: Unknown Yin REPL VM type \\\" (pr-str vm-type)\\n                         \\\"; supported: \\\"\\n                         (pr-str (vec (keys vm-constructors))))]))\\n      lang (let [lang (first args)]\\n             (if (usable-binding (select-frontend (:frontends state) lang))\\n               [(switch-lang state lang)\\n                (str \\\"Switched to \\\" lang)]\\n               [state (str \\\"Error: unavailable-parser: Yin REPL language \\\"\\n                           (pr-str lang) \\\" is not in the frontend catalog; \\\"\\n                           (installed-text (:frontends state)))]))\\n      compile [state (render-compile-output\\n                       state\\n                       (compile-command-ast state (first args)))]\\n      trust (trust-command state command args)\\n      untrust (trust-command state command args)\\n      trusted [state (str \\\"Own: \\\" (pr-str (first (:own (:link-source state))))\\n                          \\\"\\\\nTrusted: \\\"\\n                          (pr-str (vec (sort (:trusted (:admission state))))))]\\n      reset [(rebuild-session\\n               (configure-admission state (:initial-admission state))\\n               (:vm-type state))\\n             (str (get vm-labels (:vm-type state)) \\\" reset\\\")]\\n      abandon (abandon-pending state)\\n      help [state help-text]\\n      repl-state [state (format-value (repl-state state))]\\n      quit [(assoc state :running? false) \\\"Bye\\\"]\\n      telemetry [state telemetry-text]\\n      host [state host-text]\\n      [state (str \\\"Error: Unknown Yin REPL command \\\" (pr-str command))])))\\n\\n\\n(defn- eval-compiled\\n  \\\"Evaluate a program `compile-input` or `compile-source` lowered.  What\\n   its lowering left (`::frontend`: the session and the `:render` hook) is\\n   staged on the round and committed only where the round's VM state\\n   survives: when the program halts, now or when its parked require\\n   completes (`finalize-eval`).  A round that fails, rolls back, or is\\n   abandoned keeps the shell's previous `:frontend-session`.\\\"\\n  [state ast]\\n  (let [staged (::frontend (meta ast))\\n        [state' text] (eval-program (cond-> state\\n                                      staged (assoc ::frontend staged))\\n                                    ast)]\\n    [(dissoc state' ::frontend) text]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- eval-parsed*\\n  \\\"The parse dispatch of one line, against a shell with no require to\\n   re-check.\\\"\\n  [state trimmed parsed]\\n  (try\\n    (let [forms (:forms parsed)\\n          form (when (= 1 (count forms)) (first forms))]\\n      (cond\\n        (and form (command-form? form)) (handle-command state form)\\n        (and form (datom-stream? form)) (eval-program state (vec form))\\n        (and form (ast-map? form)) (eval-program state form)\\n        forms (eval-compiled state (compile-input state trimmed forms))\\n        (forms-input? state) [state (format-error (:error parsed))]\\n        :else (eval-compiled state (compile-source state trimmed))))\\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) error\\n      (let [[state' output-text] (drain-output state)]\\n        [state' (str output-text (format-error error))]))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- fold-queued\\n  \\\"Evaluate the lines retained while a require was pending, in the order\\n   they were typed, each exactly once, and join their texts.  A line that\\n   parks the shell on a require of its own stops the fold: the lines\\n   after it stay retained, in order, on the new `:pending-run`.\\\"\\n  [[state text] queued]\\n  (loop [state state\\n         text text\\n         [in & more :as queued] (seq queued)]\\n    (cond\\n      (empty? queued) [state text]\\n      (:pending-run state) [(update-in state [:pending-run :pending-lines]\\n                                       (fnil into []) queued)\\n                            text]\\n      :else (let [[state' text'] (eval-parsed* state\\n                                               (:trimmed in)\\n                                               (:parsed in))]\\n              (recur state' (str text \\\"\\\\n\\\" text') more)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- recheck-pending*\\n  \\\"One bounded re-check of the shell's `:pending-run`: serve the\\n   interpreter, run the parked VM, and either answer its value -- the\\n   round the require began in finally completes, and the lines retained\\n   while it was pending, then `input`, evaluate against it -- consult\\n   the link policy when the re-check leaves the run pending, `:checks`\\n   counting this pending run's re-checks that made no progress and\\n   resetting on one that did, or, when the re-check raised the link's\\n   refusal, return the shell to the round's base with the error as text\\n   and evaluate `input` there.  `input` is nil for the host's\\n   `recheck-pending`, which evaluates no line of its own.\\\"\\n  [state input initial-progress]\\n  (let [parked (:pending-run state)]\\n    (try\\n      (let [[state' vm' pending progress?] (drive-links state (:vm parked)\\n                                                        initial-progress)]\\n        (if (vm/halted? vm')\\n          (let [vm'' (engine/restore-initial-env (:env (:base parked)) vm')\\n                [st text] (finalize-eval state\\n                                         (cond-> (assoc state' :vm vm'')\\n                                           (:frontend parked)\\n                                           (assoc ::frontend\\n                                                  (:frontend parked)))\\n                                         (tokenize state' vm''))]\\n            (fold-queued [(assoc st :vm vm'' :pending-run nil) text]\\n                         (cond-> (vec (:pending-lines parked))\\n                           input (conj input))))\\n          (let [checks (if progress? 0 (inc (or (:checks parked) 0)))\\n                parked' (cond-> (assoc parked\\n                                       :vm vm'\\n                                       :links pending\\n                                       :checks checks)\\n                          input (update :pending-lines\\n                                        (fnil conj []) input))\\n                [st text] (drain-output (assoc state'\\n                                               :vm vm'\\n                                               :pending-run parked'))]\\n            (consult-link-policy st text (pending-text pending)))))\\n      (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) error\\n        (let [[st text] (drain-output state)\\n              d (ex-data error)]\\n          ;; retention evidence rides the shell-owned session state\\n          ;; (r11): st carries every handle and failure the parked\\n          ;; run's loaders recorded before it parked, so no exception\\n          ;; payload is read here -- a late throw from the resumed\\n          ;; run, its renderer or its result formatting cannot\\n          ;; fabricate handles, diagnostics or a Warning\\n          (fold-queued\\n            [(carry-served (assoc st\\n                                  :vm (query/discard-answers\\n                                        (carry-link-identity\\n                                          (:base parked)\\n                                          {:id-counter (::link-counter d)\\n                                           :origins (::link-origins d)})\\n                                        (:query-pair st))\\n                                  :pending-run nil)\\n                           d)\\n             (str text\\n                  (format-error error)\\n                  (dropped-lines-text (count (:pending-lines parked))))]\\n            (cond-> [] input (conj input))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn recheck-pending\\n  \\\"One re-check of the shell's `:pending-run` without an input line --\\n   the step an unattended host drives at its own cadence\\n   (yin.repl.link-policy.md section 4): serve the interpreter, run the\\n   parked VM, and consult the link policy when the run is still\\n   pending.  A re-check that completes the run folds the retained lines\\n   exactly as a typed line's completion does.  Returns `[state text]`,\\n   `text` nil when nothing is pending.  It reads no clock and takes no\\n   callback; the host decides when to call it.\\\"\\n  [state]\\n  (if (:pending-run state)\\n    (recheck-pending* state nil false)\\n    [state nil]))\\n\\n\\n(defn recheck-on-tick\\n  \\\"Advance one linker round, carrying its successor before any VM run.\\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc\\nindex d3dde756..3b7b1986 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc\\n@@ -322,10 +322,27 @@\\n \\n       :else\\n       (let [tree-address (vm/materialize-tree! handle tree)\\n-            sem-vector (:vector (linearize/project-rows tree))\\n+            ;; cc3 item 3: a claimed publication projects WITH its\\n+            ;; validated C1 rows so every image's call provenance is\\n+            ;; resolved per-occurrence at lowering time; an unclaimed\\n+            ;; publication projects exactly as before\\n+            sem-proj (if (:rows claim)\\n+                       (linearize/project-rows tree nil (:rows claim))\\n+                       (linearize/project-rows tree))\\n+            sem-vector (:vector sem-proj)\\n             sem (code/materialize-vector! handle sem-vector)\\n-            h-image (:image (derive/stack-image sem-vector))\\n-            r-image (derive/register-image sem-vector)\\n+            stack-full (when (:rows claim)\\n+                         (derive/stack-image\\n+                           sem-vector {:segment-address sem\\n+                                       :call-provenance (:call-provenance sem-proj)}))\\n+            h-image (:image (or stack-full (derive/stack-image sem-vector)))\\n+            register-full (when (:rows claim)\\n+                            (derive/register-image\\n+                              sem-vector {:segment-address sem\\n+                                          :call-provenance (:call-provenance sem-proj)}))\\n+            r-image (if register-full\\n+                      (dissoc register-full :call-provenance)\\n+                      (derive/register-image sem-vector))\\n             h (dcode/image-hash h-image)\\n             r (rcode/register-hash r-image)\\n             h-address (jing/materialize! handle h-image)\\n@@ -368,13 +385,36 @@\\n             ;; of one specification keeps its own record and table\\n             call-sites (when (:rows claim)\\n                          (let [table (jing/materialize! handle (:rows claim))\\n+                               ;; cc3 item 3: the instruction-provenance\\n+                               ;; relation of all three images, persisted\\n+                               ;; as a side artifact OUTSIDE the schema-1\\n+                               ;; manifest hash (the record names it; the\\n+                               ;; manifest never names the record)\\n+                               provenance (jing/materialize!\\n+                                            handle\\n+                                            {:yin.vm.provenance/schema 1\\n+                                             :yin.vm.provenance/root (:root tree)\\n+                                             :yin.vm.provenance/semantic\\n+                                             (mapv (fn [[pc o r p]]\\n+                                                     [sem pc o r p])\\n+                                                   (:call-provenance sem-proj))\\n+                                             :yin.vm.provenance/stack\\n+                                             (mapv (fn [row]\\n+                                                     (assoc row 0 h-address))\\n+                                                   (:call-provenance stack-full))\\n+                                             :yin.vm.provenance/register\\n+                                             (mapv (fn [row]\\n+                                                     (assoc row 0 r-address))\\n+                                                   (:call-provenance register-full))})\\n                                record {:yang.call-site/schema 1\\n                                        :yang.call-site/manifest address\\n                                        :yang.call-site/root (:root tree)\\n                                        :yang.call-site/origin (:origin claim)\\n-                                       :yang.call-site/table table}]\\n+                                       :yang.call-site/table table\\n+                                       :yang.call-site/provenance provenance}]\\n                            {:record (jing/materialize! handle record)\\n                             :table table\\n+                            :provenance provenance\\n                             :origin (:origin claim)\\n                             :root (:root tree)\\n                             :manifest address}))\\n@@ -493,6 +533,53 @@\\n                     :tree tree}))))))))))\\n \\n \\n+(defn published-instruction-provenance\\n+  \\\"The durable instruction provenance a C1-claiming publication\\n+   persisted beside its manifest (cc3 item 3): `{:root r :semantic\\n+   [[segment pc origin root path] …] :stack […] :register […]}` with\\n+   one row per call instruction of each image, every key a claimed\\n+   occurrence of the module's tree — or nil when no record verifies\\n+   or the publication claimed no C1 (legacy unannotated publications\\n+   persist none and remain fully loadable). Accepts the\\n+   `publish-module!` result map or the publication record's address,\\n+   held to the same manifest/root linkage as\\n+   `published-call-site-table`. The relation lives OUTSIDE the\\n+   schema-1 manifest hash: the record names it, the manifest never\\n+   names the record.\\\"\\n+  ([handle record-or-result]\\n+   (if (map? record-or-result)\\n+     (published-instruction-provenance handle\\n+                                       (:address record-or-result)\\n+                                       (get-in record-or-result\\n+                                               [:call-sites :record]))\\n+     (published-instruction-provenance handle nil record-or-result)))\\n+  ([handle manifest-address record-address]\\n+   (when (jing/segment-address? record-address)\\n+     (let [record (jing/get handle record-address nil)]\\n+       (when (and (map? record)\\n+                  (= 1 (:yang.call-site/schema record))\\n+                  (jing/segment-address? (:yang.call-site/manifest record))\\n+                  (or (nil? manifest-address)\\n+                      (= manifest-address (:yang.call-site/manifest record)))\\n+                  (jing/segment-address? (:yang.call-site/provenance record)))\\n+         (let [manifest (jing/get handle\\n+                                  (:yang.call-site/manifest record) nil)]\\n+           (when (and (map? manifest)\\n+                      (= (:yang.call-site/root record)\\n+                         (:yin.module/tree manifest)))\\n+             (let [relation (jing/get handle\\n+                                      (:yang.call-site/provenance record)\\n+                                      nil)]\\n+               (when (and (map? relation)\\n+                          (= 1 (:yin.vm.provenance/schema relation))\\n+                          (= (:yang.call-site/root record)\\n+                             (:yin.vm.provenance/root relation)))\\n+                 {:root (:yin.vm.provenance/root relation)\\n+                  :semantic (:yin.vm.provenance/semantic relation)\\n+                  :stack (:yin.vm.provenance/stack relation)\\n+                  :register (:yin.vm.provenance/register relation)})))))))))\\n+\\n+\\n ;; =============================================================================\\n ;; Deriving a module from indexed code (5.3)\\n ;; =============================================================================\\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc\\nindex dec0816c..7899e90c 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc\\n@@ -57,12 +57,45 @@\\n           v)))\\n \\n \\n+(defn- call-key-map\\n+  \\\"The `{semantic-pc [origin root path]}` map a `:call-provenance`\\n+   relation (`linearize/project-rows`' rows) names over vector `v` —\\n+   cc3 item 2. A row naming a pc that holds no `:call` in `v` refuses:\\n+   native provenance is reconstructed through each format's own PC\\n+   derivation, never assumed from PC equality across formats.\\\"\\n+  [v call-provenance]\\n+  (reduce (fn [m row]\\n+            (when-not (and (vector? row) (= 4 (count row))\\n+                           (integer? (nth row 0))\\n+                           (<= 0 (nth row 0)) (< (nth row 0) (count v))\\n+                           (= :call (first (nth v (nth row 0)))))\\n+              (throw (ex-info \\\"Call provenance names a non-call semantic pc\\\"\\n+                              {:rule :call-provenance, :row row})))\\n+            (assoc m (nth row 0) (subvec row 1)))\\n+          {}\\n+          call-provenance))\\n+\\n+\\n (defn stack-image\\n   \\\"Stackify A's parsed expressions, placing closure bodies in FIFO order.\\n-   Labels and diagnostics are derived solely from A.\\\"\\n-  [v]\\n-  (let [{rv :vector bodies :bodies} (resolve v)\\n+   Labels and diagnostics are derived solely from A.\\n+\\n+   The two-argument form reconstructs call provenance through the\\n+   stack derivation's own PC mapping: `opts`' `:call-provenance` is\\n+   the semantic vector's `[[pc origin root path] …]` and its\\n+   `:segment-address` is the segment coordinate the caller supplies\\n+   for this image. The result then carries `:call-provenance`\\n+   `[[segment-address pc origin root path] …]` for its `:call` pcs\\n+   and a `{:kind :call …}` side-table diagnostic at each (the existing\\n+   `:closure`/`:var` diagnostic shapes are undisturbed). The image\\n+   itself is identical with or without provenance.\\\"\\n+  ([v] (stack-image v nil))\\n+  ([v {:keys [segment-address call-provenance]}]\\n+   (let [{rv :vector bodies :bodies} (resolve v)\\n         by-start (into {} (map (juxt :start identity)) bodies)\\n+        keys-by-pc (when (some? call-provenance)\\n+                     (call-key-map v call-provenance))\\n+        call-rows (volatile! [])\\n         image (volatile! [])\\n         side (volatile! {})\\n         labels (volatile! {})\\n@@ -90,6 +123,11 @@\\n                   :var (do (diagnostic! {:kind :var :source pc})\\n                            (emit! (if (= op :load-bound) [:load-bound x y] [:load-free x])))\\n                   :call (do (doseq [child children] (walk child) (emit! [:push]))\\n+                            (when-let [[o r p] (get keys-by-pc pc)]\\n+                              (diagnostic! {:kind :call :source pc\\n+                                            :origin o :root r :path p})\\n+                              (vswap! call-rows conj\\n+                                      [segment-address (count @image) o r p]))\\n                             (emit! [:call (count y) z]))\\n                   :ffi-call (do (doseq [child children] (walk child) (emit! [:push]))\\n                                 (emit! [:ffi-call x (count y)]))\\n@@ -112,12 +150,13 @@\\n       (loop [i 0]\\n         (when-let [[l body] (get @queue i)]\\n           (mark! l) (body! body) (recur (inc i))))\\n-      {:image (mapv (fn [t]\\n-                      (case (first t)\\n-                        (:jump :branch-false) (assoc t 1 (get @labels (nth t 1)))\\n-                        :closure (assoc t 2 